      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE</p>
        <p>In these days it is neicessaty to have a skirt of me I relationship to ablouie, oraplilon%n skirt. For instance, a blouseo ckeklue tuid fawn silk cries for a bltigior fawn skis a brack A one would be distiliptlY,dowdy:4,..Y.ouNsay woor rsd and black checked Ihirt with a 'black elcirb, but an all red shirt should have a skirt of darker hue for compsmion. I saw a lady the other morn- ing whose attire had a most pleasingly harmonious gaect. Her skirt was of parma violet face cloth and she,had a soft violet silk shirt, with double pin stripes of white. I really think that blouses and shirts enjoy greater popularity than usual dur- ing this season of the year. The two styles most seen are those with the new saddle yoke, coming to a point below the shoulder, and those made of silk handkerchiefs, brought from underneath the arm., bolero fashion, and tied in a sailor's knot in front. The prevailing fashion in these garments makes for simplicity, few furbelows and frills being seen.</p>
        <p>abe ot her day I raw a charming ton inithe new cerise panne, with a fan of old lace and bunch of violets at tbe side. By the way, violets are being immensely worn, and the latet:t chic is to spray them with the cle!ici,.cs perfume of " Rhine violets," not too freely, but ja e ough to suggest the real fl3wer. .D.:epite the fact that the fur togite and velvet hat a. strongly en evidence, there are many most wildly ueseasonable millinery creations in vogue. Imagine, foreiklull and chilly November, a white tulle toque trimmed with pink end white roEeetels,- "white Inn° and ermine t-cvie, a hat of the pa:eet pink chiffon with feathers the same shade, white chiffon with pink and yellow -orchids and white feathers galore. Ree7.ntly I saw a charming hat of velvet with a largrp....4‘white few her on the left side. The brim was trimmed up above with velvA.</p>
        <p>black coat I saw yes73r:lay rPf..B of velvet. edged and trimmed with black ilk corJing. A 'black velvet hat with garzii,itin.::,n ot black and white plaid ribbon or nod bl,ck ostaitil tips, and a wlitte,lcather boa worn round the shoulders, completed the wle. The coat could be of black cloth if preferrr.ci, and tho hat of blvik felt, and the eff‘3Bt would I.m quite modish. \tally mole have seoured j7.(!ltets of fur for the -Avere w,"7. her, but it rafly seems a long Limo coining. One I the other day was in handsome bip.cit fnricros3ed over tho.boewit with a high collar luuh would prove very n - stormy weather.</p>
        <p>s</p>
        <p>At this season of the year our thoughcs naturally go out to our friends and the present they will find useful for ornament and wear. Gloves are always a charming present, as one can never have too many. But many people delight in curios, and certainly nothing merits the title of curious more than the Chinese productions. The most up-to-date silver pencil-casee are flat, and fitted with flat cedar pencils, and this shape can be had in gold too, but of course they are much more costly. Beautiful purses and card-cases in morocco are fastened with a jewelled stud; and rxme only ornamented with graduated rows of waved rims of silver are particularly neat. Photograph-frames grow more and more beautiful each year; some in turquoise-blue rimmed with silver, with a true- lov'er's-knot on the top, are loyely— Thy are round in form to suit the favourite shaped photo, and so are some very handsome ones in Algerian cnyx, with fancy gilt rims decorated with Louis- XV. bows.</p>
        <p>Very useful for decorative mottoes are cotton wool letters. These are easiest made by pasting white paper at the back of the sheet of cotton- wool, sketching the letters on the paper, " cutting them out with sharp scissors, and lightly floffini; up the wool with the fingers. A sprinkle of frost- powder is an improvement. A pretty effect can be produced by touching any garlandi or wreath- ings of holly here and there with gain and drop- ping on each moist spot a little " snowflake " of wool. Big balls of holly made on the principle of a cowelip-nall and thus strewn with snowflakes look very well hung in the centre of an archway or window from a chandelier either by a length of thin holly wreathing or a bright red cord wound round with ivy. Ohildron's hoops (in the smallest sizes) make good foundations for holly wreaths for wall decorations, and barrel hoops may be used for the same purpose. Strips of cloth on which Christ- mas greetings have been boldly painted, will also help to adorn a long expanse of wall very success- fully. The leather-cloth can be edged with a band of holly leaves sewn on green calico or strong brown paper. I may observe that I hope soon to give you something relative to fancy dresses. Your loving, Jsrann.</p>
        <p>, / i , „.:,,,,, A, ../,), 07 ......v. ~.!,..; iik ~,o ~k, ~,,,, ph,,, V, ql• '•::( '',,'4,' ~.'/,q/A oks-\• ••.' t '''.,z/ I§A, ',.;.•'.:i' 7/9///Avi!,-,•' - /,,:r,e7:-.2;f1• :-.1-4"-!...-://':"'.' • ‘9.7./b////41 Pl' t .- ,C.':' Ca ' ll'e.4l.:A\k b:i',,,', ) 11/ - , L. ~,, ~, , / 1.-d 4 i rid,r,</p>
        <p>_ /'l' .. ,:, k_‘..,:z,t,._ _,...,,,,,,0' •!, • . it-q;.:o,blij.'•,--: '' ' . i, .tr ''"'. ~. ,'-',77'-- - '''.- • , : '-i', ~:. , ,„' • &lt; , .pal.,-.;‘,.,:,..,-:,-,, -- -..-, ,•, ~,,...-• ,/;‘,.., -,. . , . - • ~-,. ~ .•,,, ;„' , •-.,'A,.". :-2- . -- ~, ' ~,,;,,,,,. , /./7/ -i, ' - - • ' .-• ~,,,,.,,././. i4/', • ,/,'„:,-i'/,' ,‘•.• - • ' - ~4.!4:', 7,-;// ',,•:,,;;'#/-''...-?,:'' : - •-,_:- ' -'. '-'41:,',.. ,;//i,:.; // • 1 ~..?; ./.. i 4 .. , ...:. ~ ~ ~,</p>
