      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</head>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>- —•—• - - • - - For some .ieason it seeins difficult to realise that; we are once more on the threshold of Christmas, so welcome for its sessonkble festivities, family gatnerings, and other pleasant associations. "The Church Montnly" for 'December tells of two ways ef keeping Christmas. The first is a quotation from Themac Carlyle waerein he says "All mortals are tumblirg about iu a state of drunken saturnalia, delirium, or guard-delirium, accord ing to their several eorts,a very strange method of thank- ing God for sßnding them a Redeemer." Th tr. of course was Carlyle's way of looking at most things, but it is no reason, and as.a matter of fact mere is now very little cause, for such gloomy and exaggerated views. sec&amp;ntl is the Editors' advice. Christmas, be , 11:8, is the season when tile scattered members of families usually make an effort to spend a day or two with the old folks at borne. But in nearly every parish some aged pilgrim may be fogncl who expects nobody for the simple reason that -h...'•Or she has outlived the family, or the few survivors are too far away to make the journey. It is this solitary ueighbour whom the E itor bids us to remember, and it is well to think that few, who know of such a case ,neglect this Christmas</p>
        <p>Christmas Day is said to tall less often on a Monday , than it does on Borne other days of the week. Accord- ing to an expert in these calmilatioas,it falls on Sunday, Tuesday, or Thursday forty-eight times, and on Friday and Saturday fifty-seven times, for every fitty-six times it falls on a Monday or a Wednesday. This irregularity is due to some 21ight error remaining between our calen- dar year and the actual solar year. Everyone knows that the one is 365 days, bat the solar year is 385 days 5 hours, 48 minutes. and 46 seconds. This difference is more than made good by the added day every leap year, and this error in turn is adjusted as near as It can he,by foregoing one leap year in a century. Next year, though divisible by four is cot a leap year —but after all these c.,rrections thsro still remains an error in our,calandar system, and hence the) pecu- liarity that Christmas Day falls more often on some days than on others. It must of coarse be remembered that the nineteenth century has more than twelve months as yet to run, though there are still many people who believe that inext year will commence a new century. This mistaken notion, -mostly-arises from the analogy of a man's age,and people say that anyone born on January Ist, 1800, will, if still living, be 100 years old the first of next month, and will therefore commence his hundred and lint year. But in our calendar we can only count the beginning of things as the year one, and therefore the old century ends with the year 1900. Christmas in Paris has none of the importance which attaeltes to it in other places, far less importance in fact than it , any other part of France. To most Parisians Christmas is simply known as the season of presents. Christmas Day is of course a holiday, it is too the great occasion of family re-unions,the big family dinner, and heavy digestions, but otherwise there is but little enjoyment. Around the beautiful blooms of the black hellebore, known distinctivelg as Christmas roses, much legendary lot° has gathered.(. There is One especially pretty legend to the effect that this Christmas rose first flowered in the gardens of Heaven, when the angels knew it as the " rose of love." When Adam and Eve were driven from Eden, leaving behind them all the blossoms which they had loved to tend, out into a waste world, covered with snow, the pitying angels sought and obtained permis- sion to give them one flower this" rose of love," as a sign of the Divine love, and thereupon it blossomed amid the snow. It is a pretty story, and other like legends can be read in the various works that have been published, dealing with the fascinating subject of plant and flower lore.</p>
        <p>The root of the black hellebore was formerly supposed to have mrdicinal qualities of high value, especially in the cz.,s^ of mental disease. The root is still imported to some small extent from Germany, for we in medicinal practice, but the idea that it will cure mental maladies has long been exploded. The traditional association however, of the drug and naadness, crops up occasionally in literature.</p>
        <p>Reticules are well to the fore, and numbers of women exhibit pretty little bags slung on their wrists, in the evening. These are Usually built nf silk, velvet, satin, or chiffon,in cllour to maitcl the ;,owri.h.nd adorned with frills of old lace. They prove most useful, for they hold handkerchief, opera glasses, fan, and provide a safe restiog place for ones' gloves during dinner, thereby saving ones' neighbour from the all Luo customary dive under the table.</p>
        <p>Notwithanndine the papolarl..v of mauve,and m‘stie and every other delicate cc! ,ur, bla,k in.i white gowns are very chic and modiah. Sometimes a touch of colour is introduce ; in the shape of a narrow waistbelt of turquols3 blue, or rose piuk, or a breast knot of the</p>
        <p>wearers' faroutite flower, without taking from the style. Black crepe de chine is a lovely fabric to com- bine with white satin and black guipure. Imagine a g ,wn of this, the skirt quite plain over the hips, and falling in deep points back and front over an underhkirt of white -satin, veiled in black guipure. The bodice softly draped round the figure from a yoke of tucked white mousseline de soie, set in scallops of the black guipure over white Satin. Rosettes of black velvet bebe ribbon finishing off tho bodice at the left side, and also the wai-t hind, of twisted crepe de chine. The incidents repotted to have occurred at a file in Re adin.e, Pennsylvania, when 500 girls are stated to have jumped from windows, must have revived in many minds the wonder that precautions are seldom takec to guard against loss of life en such occasions. Lifts and escapes of simple construction have been in- venteefor the purpose, but there seems to be little de. trend for them, and a man who constructed a very use- ful apparatus of the kind, only succeeded in losing money, over it. Everybody seems to admit in the abAract the importance of such eafeguards,but scarcely anybody appears to comtemplato the possibility of their buin g useful in the case of his own establishment. Every other person one meets seems to be suffering with .a cold, a state of aft tscare,_,iy to be mareelled That a cold contracted frequ: ritly lasts until late the spring is a fact kr ,cu te most of us, and we eh-uld therefore do our best t ward off a threatened attack,by taking ourselves in ha A glassful of lemonade taken hot es noß‘ible a frequently, ward c.ff a cold. The juice of t t hunlui be sqUeed toto a tumbler, filled up w wt..ter acd SweeicnF3 to taste.</p>
        <p>D titri</p>
        <p>j!4A D 6 E."</p>
        <p>MERIi REWARDED.</p>
        <p>That real merit is the outstanding feature of Chivers Gold Medaal Jellies is a statement which is capable of asy and abundant proof. The fact that so many thou- sands of consumers continue to buy them year after sear speaks volumes for their worth, and is a reward which only attaches to merit. Chivers' Jellies are pre- pared in a healthy Cambridges%ire vii age, by up-to-date methor4, with the most scrupulous cleanliness and care and flavoured with ripe fruit juices. The high-class quality and delicious fruity flavour of these dainties gives immense satisfactioe all round. Here is a tribute from a Free Church Minicer " We /lase tried mans' other kinds of Jelly, but have long decided to speculate no more, as we have been disappointed each time. We really find none like Chivers.' " A Wholesale Grocer writes : " I have just been testing other Jellies which have been s,nt,to me for trial, and have decided that no other Jelly canrhompare with Chivera' both far texture and flavour." k lady says "Without a fault, elm ply perfectitn." Anctber Lady remarks : " I have riot found any so good as yours." Here is the testimony of The Lancet : "An excellent Jelly possessing a flavour of quite a natural character." Sold by :Grocers and Stores in paclcetc, Fralf-pints 21d., Pints 4id., Quarts Bd. A tree sampleiwill be sant on receipt of post-c.rd. S. Chivers &amp; arm, Elston, Cam- bridge. Please mention this paper.</p>
