      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>NOBLER THAN REVENGE.</head>
        <p>NOBLER THAN REVENGE.</p>
        <p>By ESAIt STUART,</p>
        <p>Author of "In the Dark," "Inscrutable,* Woman of Forty," "Sara's Mother," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XII.</p>
        <p>When Alec left Ruby he meditated long on what</p>
        <p>he had done. He had taken a boid -step and must not draw back. He must save Ruby in spite of her father, and in spite of these strange rela- tions. What could be the reason which com- pelled Sir Harry to do the will of these two men ? Certainly be was fond, nay, very fond, of his child. Could he (Alec) run away with Ruby? The idea was not pleasant, and.went against all Alec's honourable feelings, but surely there must be some cases where it was impossible to act straightforwardly. Ruby must be saved. There were yet two hours before supper time, and an idea struck him that be would go to the town and make a few inquiries about trains. If the worst came to the worst be would take Ruby to a cousin of his adopted mother and ask her to take care of the girl till such a time as he could get Sir Harry's consent to an engagement. It was a bold idea, but Alec could think of nothing else. Would Ruby consent? In her present frame of mind she would certainly do anything rather than be forced into James Felton's company. Alec went to the stables and called for Bob, but at that moment Bob was indoors doing his work, and not W ishing to delay longer Alec strode off in the direction of Huckdale. It would take him less than half an hour to walk there, and he would be back in plenty of time for supper. Mrs. Spencer, The lady in question, lived in London, and he would get an answer, if possible; then he must see Ruby that evening and arrange with her that if her father did not give in she would let him manage for her. It was all difficult. But had not Sir harry called him Ruby's guardian, and, if so, was he not bound to do the best he could for her? Full of these possibilities he started forth, with- out wasting more time. The moon was rising, and the road was straight when once one got out of the Yewdale estate. Alec enjoyed the walk, that is the mere physical exercise. Moreover, he made a picture of Ruby as his wife, how they would live very quietly on his earnings. Would it be possible? But surely her father would help them till such a time astAlec would enter into his inheritance. It seemed all so symmetrical, so like a novel that Alec smilid at the pictures which succeeded each other so quickly and so easily. A sweet, lovable wife, a'small home, with comfort if with no luxury, and occasional visits to Fewdale when Ruby required change of air. Ruby would soon learn to be happy in a home of her own, and she must learn to know more of the ways of the world than she knew at pre: ent. When he reached Huckdale he hurried to the post- office. There was a young and talkative woman serving at the counter. Alec wrote his form and handed it in to her. "It isn't often, sir, we take so many telegrams from the same place. As I was saying to my aunt just now, Yewdaie Hall ain't often so lively in the telegram line, and here's another." "A reply paid," said Alec. "I will wait for it. If it does not come in time, I will call for it to- morrow early." "Very well, sir. It is a long walk fol. you. Couldn't I send it up for you ? " "No thank you," said Alec, wishing the woman were not so friendly and so curious. He had better have written, but he wanted to save time as he had so few days in which to settle everything, It was very strange that in such a short time so much had happened • indeed, he felt that he had been at Yewdale Hail a long time instead of a few days. He waited about the little house as long as he could, hut as no answer came he thought it better to go home; his absence might be noticed. As it was he had to hurry, and when he entered the estate he walked still more quickly as just beyond the gate be had to go through a plantation of firs which were rather thick, and which now were dark and melancholy-looking. It was at this moment that his mind suddenly recurred to his former adventure. He did not in the least fear a repetition of it, for he himself could • not possibly have been intended as the victim ; but still he should have liked to clear up the mystery. e entered the dark plantation and looked into the black depth, wondering what Ruby would say if e knew that he had been attacked on his first At that very moment there was a quick rast!e in the underwood, and a bullet whizzed past him, missing him by a hair's breadth. Alec was furious, and without a moment's thought dashed . into the wood, just able to see a retreating figure. It wes a common man, that he could be sure of, 'broad: back and fustian coat, but further he could .:reof eke sure of. It had all happened in so short that he found himself half through the when he remembered that he could not possi- bly hope to dodge a native, who would know all the twists and escape. Already he could see nothing, and he thought that the better part of valour would be to retrace his steps and this time appeal to the squire. It took him some time to find his way out of the wood, and when at last he did so he knew that it was very late, and he knew also that supper must be almost over. When he came into the hall he found that the front door was shut, and in answer to the bell the old servant appeared. "I beg your pardon, sir, but I was told you was out for the night." "Indeed, who could have thought that ? " "Bob did say that he was sure that you would come." Alec did not stop to answer, but walked boldly into the hall. James Felton exclaimed: "Well, Beauworth, I thought that something had happened to you as you did not turn up." Sir Harry lbarely looked up, and Alec saw that he was much disturbed. Of course, Ruby had been speaking to him. "Something did happen," said Alec, coldly, then turning to the squire, he continued: "I have had a dastardly attack made upon me. In the fir-wood a man shot at me Ho missed me by almost nothing." "My dear Beauworth, lam very sorry. It must have been that you were mistaken for a poacher. I have been so plagued with them that one of the keepers has determined to make an example." - "Re can't imagine that a poacher walks in the middle of the path, straying neither to the right nor to the left. "Of course not. I cannot imagine how it can have happened." "Neither need he run away when he is-" "So he ran, did he, that showed a bad con- science," said James. "We mast get to the bottom of it," said Sir Harry. An awkward silence fell on the party. Mr. Felton broke it. "So Mies Ruby has a headache," he said, as if he were making an ordinary remark. Alec felt his colour rising. Ruby's name should not be mentioned by people who could insult her; but it would not be long before it would be out of their power to annoy her. For prudence sake he said nothing, but went to the other end of the hall to examine some old armour. James followed him. "Will you come to the lailliard-room ? "suggested James. "'Thank you, I have letters to write," replied Alec. "I will go to my room." "You don't seem sociable," James said, sneeringly. "You want a little dose of the colonies." "I am more than satisfied with England." "Oh, you will not be so satisfied when a certain young lady has left it." "If you are speaking of Miss Beauworth I prefer not to discuss her." "As you like; but you can't pretend to say that you have not heard that Ruby and I ~are to be married shortly." "I have heard it, but I do not believe that you can marry a girl against her will." "How do you know that? The grapes are sour." "You are doing an unwarrantable thing. I don't know what her father's motive may be, but--" "That is just it. You know nothing about it or yen would brag less." "Will you give her tip? For I shall try to thwart you as much as I can, and I give you due notice.</p>
        <p>James laughed. "I do not fear you. Do your worst." The squire now came forward looking pale and troubled.</p>
        <p>"I think, Beanworth, that it is no use arguing it with James. I have told you all this was settled long ago. It is hardly honourable of you to try to undermine Ruby's obedience." . "I :1,1341=1 if you thiu:k that I have .aeted</p>
        <p>wrongly, nut i. cannot see a girt sacrificed without trying to-"</p>
        <p>"Come, come," said Mr. Felton, 'interrupting he trio. "Away dull care. Come and have a game of cards. Let us leave all discussion till to- morrow."</p>
        <p>"I think that, under the circumstances, I would prister.fting away to-morrow," said Alec. • The soiuire looked distressed. "You must please yourself, Beauworth, but you had better wait till-"</p>
        <p>"Thank you, my mind is made up." Alec was angry. He felt that there was some- thing underneath the whole scene which he could not understand, and that he had better try to persuade Ruby to hold out till such a time as he could claim her. But how was he to see Ruby again? He knew that the squire locked the door in the upstair passage, and that the little door lead- ing to the stairs was also locked. If he`could find Bob he might, perhaps, bribe old Nancy, but Bob had work to do and did not appear at this time. He would go to his room and try to wait till he could ask the old butler. for Bob. He walked slowly up the stairs and turned the corner. At this moment the squire overtook him. "Bea;uwortb, wait a moment. I want to tell you. that you are playing with fire. You must not make James angry. He is--" "I shall not see him again; indeed, I mean to go before breakfast, so I must say good-bye to you uow. Sir Harry." "You are wrong to take this as a personal matter. • In the future, when you are master here, you can do as you like; till then-" ,"I told you that I do not count on being master of Yevvdale. You may marry or--" "You do not repent about Ruby ? " "No, I do not repent. I would willingly do more for her than her own father will do."</p>
        <p>"Her own' father," he repeated, slowly. "You do not understand; it is because,l love Ruby that I have tried to save her."</p>
        <p>"I have told Ruby what I think of it." "You have destroyed her peace of mind, and you can do her no.good as far as James is concerned." "I would protect her in spite of him. I shall try even now." The two men parted, but Alec remembered after- wards that Sir Harry suddenly came back a few steps after he had partly gone downstairs, but after a slight hesitation he went on his way. Alec walked on towards his own room, wondering many things, chiefly whether it were possible to• save Ruby. He would go early the next morning to the town, and see if there was an answer to his telegram; then he would, somehow, get Ruby to come and meet him under Bob's escort, and he would himself take her to a safe refuge till her father relented. He must relent when he saw that Ruby was in earnest. He reached his bedroom door, and saw that there was a cheerful fire burning on the hearth. Bob must have seen to that. Otherwise there as not a sound, as his pascage was far removed from the lower room where the Feltons were playing billiards. Ruby was above them. If only his room had been near hers, he might have found some way of communicating. His one hope was that Bob would come in. He wanted to tell him about the attack of the keeper, so-called, though Alen did not believe in that idea. It was much more likely that Bob's friends had again mistaken him. Had not Bob begged him to go away? He must have had some idea that this attempt would be made. Bob was certainly a sharp lad, and his warnings de- served attention. Well, the next day Alec would shake the dust off his feet; and when once Ruby was safely out of the clutches of these three men, he would keep a safe distance from Yewdale. Alec closed the door of his room rather wearily. It had been an exciting day. Now it was over, and he should never see these men again. He walked towards his dressing-table, and again he paused. Should he go Lack, and try to get an interview with Ruby this evening? But no, it was safer for her to be patient. He walked a few steps further, and suddenly felt the floor give way beneath his feet. He coiled out, tried to clutch at some object, but there was none near at hand. He felt himself hurled somewhere into black space, he felt darkness around him; then he knew that he was falling heavily somewhere, and he remembered no more.</p>
        <p>• • • That evening Bob Was kept employed by James Felton in the billiard-room, and it was therefore impossible for him to get away early. Indeed, it was past twelve o'clock when at last he was released.</p>
        <p>"Off with you, young clod-hopper. The squire has put you down here so that we can keep an eye on you. No loitering, for I shall want you early to-morroiv in the woods."</p>
        <p>Bob did not want to say: "Yes, sir," but he did. 7 He wanted to go and see if Mr. Alec had gone to his room, as Rudd had said, but he dared not do so till all was quiet. "At all events, they have been here," said Bob, 'zo himself, "and he's come home safe; but he ought to cut and rim."</p>
        <p>Bob was very sleepy; he slunk into the tiny room where he had put a shake-down for himself, deter- mined to keep awake till the Feltons were both asleep. Bob was human, however, and hardly had he stretched himself full-length on the mattress than he seemed t.q.4ll,into a sound slumber. He was not even aware of a stealthy step outside his door, nor did he hear the door open as James Felton muttered: "The boy is sleeping like a top." "NO fear of him; he is stupid," said the father. "Not so sure; he looks rather too sharp at things." "You judge the clod-hoppers by yourself. What about the squire? " "He's all right, but a greater fool it rwould be hard to find. You didn't warn me that he would have qualms." "It's all the girl. No accounting for girls. She's got round him." "She won't get round me," chuckled the young man.</p>
        <p>"I reckon she won't," answered his father, with a smile, "no one ever got round you, James—at least, I never knew that individual." __</p>
        <p>"It's the squire that is a fool, for it was all as neat as a--"</p>
        <p>"Hark!. What was that noise ? " "Nothing, governor; don't be nervous, for the game is trapped as tight as a trivet." "Did you see that all was right ? " "Of course I did, when you were telling the squire that two and two made four." "But after, James? " "Oh, the squire must do the rest. A week from now we shall be on the high seas. Miss Ruby must wake haste to get over her objections." "Much better leave her here after the wedding." *Can't trust her. You should have seen what a little spitfire she can be!" "The squire played too deep a game. Beauworth has made complications." "Old fogeys always think everyone has made a mull except themselves. I got the banns all right, read in a City church, and we were represented by a couple not much like me and Ruby. But the curate does the weddings and the vicar bags the money. It's uncommonly easy to get married in England." "The berths are all right. lam trying to get the squire to come too." "Best leave him-alone. I'm off to bed," said James.</p>
        <p>"Well, I'm sleepy." When Bob awoke he felt much ashamed of him- self for having given way to sleep before making sure that all was right. He stood up and listened. No sound was to be heard; but it was moonlight, and he could see without lighting a stable lantern which he had brought with him. He crept out and stood in the passage. No sound came from there. The Feltons must have retired to bed at last. Bob crept into the passage, and listened again. He knew that on no account must he be heard going out. It was just possible that James Felton was still out of doors; but it was not likely, as it must be late.</p>
        <p>He then tried the garden door. It was not locked. He knew that the Feltons used it; they did not lock it, as there was no one but themselves in that wing. Bob opened it very softly, and stole out. Once out of the house, he breathed more freely, and treading on the grass, he ran round to the front, by which path he could get to Mr. Beau- worth's window. In three minutes he was on the window-ledge. The window was unfastened. He pushed it open, and let himself into the room. His first glance at the bed revealed that it was empty. No one was in the bed, nor had anyone been in it since it had been made in the morning. Bob was petrified for the first moment, and remained rooted to the spot. Then he approached nearer, and again stared at the bed. No looking could alter the fact that no one had been in that bed, and that no one was in it now. "Mr. Alec," said Bob, softly, as if his friend would answer from some hidden depth of the bed. Then Bob looked round again. Mr. Alec's portmanteau was on the floor, and it was ready packed. Then he had gone; but, no, he would surely have taken .his Yuggage with him. Perhaps he had gone in a hurry, and had 'salt to come on. Bob tuned. it all wan</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>mind, and could make nothing of tha fads berm'. him.</p>
        <p>"The devil has beat me," said Bob, to himself,' "unless Mr. Alec took fright and cut for it; but he would have told me. He's gone, that's &amp;Lie and certain."</p>
        <p>Bob looked into the'big cupboard. There was nothing, all was silent. Only the moonlight streamed into the room, looking calm and unmind- ful of the man who was not there. Bob felt angry. He had meant Mr. Alec to go, but not without his knowing. ' At last he felt that he must do something. He shut the window, and determined to see if he could hear any sound within the house. He went to the door 4nd found it locked, but from the outside, so he must return the way he had come.</p>
        <p>Theie-was nothing to do this evening. Perhaps it was all right: He would hear in the morning what the butler knew about it, and Miss Ruby, who had been so unhappy yesterday, of course she would have heard. Turning it over in all ways, Bob could form no theory as to Alec's disappear- ance. At last he crept back to his room, and threw himself down on the mattress. He could not sleep, he could only conjecture. Well, he would find him somehow.</p>
        <p>It was getting light when Bob heard a slight noise coming from the Feltons' room. He listened. Yes, there were steps, soft steps, passing his door. The garden door was opened, Just as Bob himself had opened and closed it again. Bob started up and listened. He waited thus ten minutes,. then he heard the door open again. This time he was ready. When the steps had passed his door, he softly opened it and looked out. It was Mr. James creeping back to his room. Then he shut his door, and all was silent again till it was time to get up. There was no need for Rudd to knock twice at Bob's door; he was longing to hear what he knew.</p>
        <p>"Mr. 13 an vorthl Oh, he's gone all in a hurry. Mr. James came to tell me last night that there had be n some unpleasant words, and that he said he world go, and that I was to send his bag after him I, -da:."</p>
        <p>"T. at's queer," said Bob; "and didn't he say nothir gto you?" _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>bui,-Kr. James said that he left me half-a- soverc ign, and was sorry not to say good-bye to me."</p>
        <p>"Didn't he say nothing about me ? " asked Bob. "About you! Why, gents don't think of the likes of you, Bob! Come, make haste, and clean Mr. James's boots. He's going out, and says that he's got important business." "Has he? " said Bob. "I don't care for he." "Don't you talk of your betters; got along with you. They are all off soon." "Where is Mr. Alec's luggage going?" asked Bob, _later on.</p>
        <p>"Mr. James is going to take it to the station after breakfast."</p>
        <p>"Am Ito go too? " Bob thought he saw a means of knowing more. "I'll ask. Someone will have to go." Bob had plenty to do with boots and knives and odd jobs, so it was not till much later that he got a chance of finding Nancy, when she came down to fetch some milk for breakfast.</p>
        <p>"Where is Mr. Alec, /si.ancy, and does Miss Ituby_w:Fh to see him ? " '</p>
        <p>"Whatever are you saying! Miss Ruby is a deal 6? trouble. She said that I was to ask you, Bob, about Mr. Alec,"</p>
        <p>"He's gone," said Bob. "Gone! No, for sure, for he told her he would see her to-day, and I was to go to the foot of the stairs at nine o'clock. Loll I went, but he never came. The squire would send me right off if lie knew, but Miss Ruby does fret so." "Then she didn't know he was gone ?" "Snow it, poor darling, why, she's crying her heart out about Mr. James."</p>
        <p>"Sweet on her, is he ? " asked Bob, who was not sentimental.</p>
        <p>"And going to marry her too! That's what she wants .Mr. Alec for. He promised to save her." "But he's gone," repeated Bob. "Tell her that I'll do anything she tells me, for I was a particular friend of Mr. Alec's. You'll tell her, won't you, Nancy? " "Gone! Never! Why, t don't understand it, and he making so many promises." "Bob, Bob! " called out James Felton, and Bob rushed away to meet the enemy, as the boy called him to himself. Bob's face only expressed stupidity. "Hulloa, youngster 1 I want you to go to the village and get the post-mistress to give you any letters or telegrams she may have. So con- foundedly slow,,people are here, and then you can meet me at thetstation and come back with me." "Yes' sir," said :Bob, secretly delighted. At the station he would hear if Mr. Alec had really gone. He could make no guesses, but he felt very uneasy. There was, of course, a chance that Mr. Alec ha&amp; been alarmed and had taken Bob's advice and had cut, but it did not seem likely that between supper and the next morning he had made off unseen by-anyone.</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
