      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>NEWS OF VIE GAME.</head>
        <p>NEWS OF VIE GAME.</p>
        <p>As was ex;::, cted after the defeat of Mr. Morral the Liverpool championship has once more bee won by Mr. Cain, his score in the final with Mr, Nixon being--Cain 3. Nixon nil, drawn 1. Morrall was distinctly unfortunate in losing upon a tech- nicality, nod we are suie Mr. Cain would have pre- ferred to fiL.lht out the heat Under the rules, he had no option, as when his opponent undulN delayed to make his move he was obliged to enforce the penalty, which in this case was the forfeiture of the gam. r Cain takes the title and first prize, and the second prize goes to Mr. Nixon.</p>
        <p>MiSs Helen Keller, says the "Liverpool Mercury," is one of the wonders of the age. She is an Ameri- can lady, who was born deaf, dumb, and blind, but so successfully have her physical disadvantages been combated that she can now speak, and is per- fectly well- understood by her friends, and this although is incapable of distinguishing the slightest sound. One of her amusements is draughts, in which she i very proficient, and we are not for wrong in describing her as. the most remarkable drauvhts player, that ever lived. But this is only a minor ,accomplishment. She swims, sculls, cycles, wrestle, and rides a horse. She is an expert typist, and not long ngo used a typewriter when undergoing an examination in classio:. atl higher mathematics. In this examination die was successful, and it entitled ter to enter Ba'icliff, College, which is in some way connected with Harvard University.</p>
      </div>
