      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>LISERAL, CLUB.</head>
        <p>LISERAL, CLUB.</p>
        <p>Presentation to Mr. and Mrs. E. J. Mitchell.</p>
        <p>The members of the Warrington Liberal Club have shown their appreciation of the fifteen yeas e of loyal and devoted service of the late manager and his wife, Mr. and Mrs. F. J. Mitchell in a practical way for on Monday evening they were the recipients of a purse of gold suilscrioed for by 150 members. The meeting at whioll this interesting presentation took place, was held in, the Committee Room, Mr. J. Albinson presiding. Amongst others present in aidition to Mr and Nit's. Mitchell, were Messrs W Robeets,G Wallingtort.W Ford, S Beaman, W Pickup, J Locker, W Hayes, La Auckland, W Critchley, and J Shaw. Mr. W. ROBERTS made the presentation on behalf of the subscribers. lie said dear, when be was asked to occupy teat position he felt it was an honour to do so. (Elear, hear.) tie was sure he was not only speaking the sentiments of the members present, bat of the 150 sub•cribers,when he said that Mr. Mitchell's resignation came to them with a feeling of surprise and regret. There were a number of those present who could look hack upon the fifteen years connection that Mr. Michell had had with the club with a grear amount of pleasure and satisfaction. (Hear, Elev..) He Cani, to them with the highest testimonials and recommend- ations and he (the speaker) was pleased to say he was unanimously appointed by the committee. During the whole of the fifteen years Mr Mitchell, his wife and family bad held the confilence, the gocclx,ll, and the resp,et of the members of the club and committee (Hear, hear.) Speaking as one who had served the club de many capacities he believed with the utmost sincerity that in Mr Mitchell they had had a good, honest and faithful servant—(hear, hear)—and one who had deserved the confidence and respect of teem al.. There were many who thought it would be ungrateful of them to aliow him and his wife to leave without some expres- sion of the hearty good feeling which they all fe t towards them. They regretted their separation from them as officers of the club, bet they were glad to know that they should have Mr. and Mrs. Mitehell amongst them, as they were still resident in the tewn and he believed that in Mr. Michell they would have one who would always be willing to rendlr all the service he could both to the Liberal Club and the Liberal party in the future. (136ar, hear.) He thought that the gift which was a purse of gold conrainine£l4,lo. was a .very creditable one and spoke vohemes for the kindly feeling eptertained for him and his family. In asking Mr. and Mrs. Mitchell's acceptance of the testimonial allow him to say that they sincerely regretted their resignation and hoped that in years to come they would centinue to enjoy their fellowship, friendship and esteem. Fie thought the happy hours they had spent with Mr. Mitceell in the club eyelid ever be remembered by him with the liveliest satisfaction and they wished him every possible happiness in his future life. (Applause.) Mr. G. WALLINGTON agreed with Mr.RePerts in every- thing he had said. Mr. Mitchell bad always held the confidence of everyone with whom he had come into contact and he wishea him success in the future. (Ap- plause.) Mr. BEAMAN said they very much regretted that Mr. and Mrs. Mitchell were leaving them and wished thorn every succ,ss in their new sphere of life. Mr. PICKUP, one of the secretaries of the committee appointed to carry out the presentation, said he had re- ceived the greatest courtesy when he asked for subscr;p- Lions. Ile thought it was only right and prover that the presentation should be made, and hoped Mr. and Mrs. Mitchell would have a brighter future and Nuccess in their new sphere of labour. Mr. ilcliTcHeLL in reply, tendered his heart-felt thanks to the subscribers for their kindnees in making the pre- sentation. He was very pleased to think that 150 mem- bers of the Liberal Club had cheerfully subset:bed for a testimonial to him and his wife. It was certainly very gratifying. Be was pleased to see that some of the gentlemen who had taken an active Part in the presenta- tion were members of the club when he was appointed fifteen years ago, as it testified to the coefidence they had paced in him during that time. With regard to the financial position of the club, when he was appointed, the profi's for the year amounted to £64 which was con- siderel to be unprecedented;now they amounted to about £l5O a year. He did not claim the credit for that, but he had been as economical as poesible. In conclusion he thanked them for the kindnese they had bestowed upon him and hoped the gond feelings that had Existed while he had been at the club, would continue to exist. It would be a source of joy and comfort to him to think that 150 members of the club Showed their regard for him and his family by subscribing to that purse of gold. (Applause.) Mrs, MITCHELL briefly returned thanks. Votes of thanks to tee Chairman for presidine and to the secretaries of the committee. Mr. W Pickup and Mr. L Aukland, were accordeci,land the proceedings ter- minated with the singing of" He's j fly good fellow."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>FATAL ACCIDENT AT THE WORKHOUSE INFIRMARY' A WIDOW'S CLAIM FOR THE LOSS OF A SON.</head>
        <p>THE FATAL ACCIDENT AT THE WORKHOUSE INFIRMARY' A WIDOW'S CLAIM FOR THE LOSS OF A SON.</p>
        <p>Before his Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes at the Warrington County Court last Thursday, Sarah Cooper, of 61, Cummings-street, Pentonville-toad , London, widow, claimed £3OO compensation from Charles W. Davenport. builder, Stockton Heath, for the loss of her son who wasialled at the new Workhouse Infirmary at Warrington on the 3rd October. Mr. Browne represented the applicant and Mr. Acton, barrister of Manchester (instructed by Mr. Willett) appeared for the respondent. Mr. Winstanley, of Park-place, plumber, &amp;c., was a third party to the action,he being a sub-contractor on the works and the employer of the deceased. He was represented by Mt. rullock of Manchester. Mr. Acton said the matter had been under careful consideration, and he did not see any ground for resisting an award, such an amount as his. Flonnir thought fit to give. He understood that Mr. Tullock had seen grounds upon • which he thought he could successfully resist the claim. He, however propoced to leave the matter entirely in his Honour's discretion. • His Honour suggested that they had better agree upon the amount, and have it filtd as an agreement. not as an award. , Mr. Acton (after a short consultation with Mr. 'Browne) said Mr. Browne bad met them in a reasonable spirit,and they agreed that £260 should be taken as the amount of the damages, subject to His Honour's approval. , Mr. Tulloek applied for leave to resist the claim, and after a long legal areurnent his Honour assented. Hoary haws Cooper. 180, St. Paul's-road. Highbury, , London, brother of the deceased man, said his mother Sarah Cooper was 62 years of age and was a cripple. IShe was solely dependent upon the dereated for her rnaintenance.—Cross-examined by Mr. Tullock : The deceased on the average had sent his mother 15a a week Witness had made his mtither little preaents—no: a regu- lar weekly allowance—fohen she bad been el 03 an average the assistance to his mother would amount to half-a-crown a wetk. Herbert James Green, in the employ of Messrs. William and Segar Owen as clerk of works of he new infirmary, produced the contract made between the Warrington Guardians and Mr. Davenport for toe erection of the Infirmary which stated that Mr. Daven- port was reponsible for tile whole of the work. Mr. Tull .ck addressine His Honour said he took it from what had been stated by the deceased's brothers, that the mother was not wholly dependent upon the de- ceastd, and she were :only partially ;dependent, his tioaour was entitled to review the facts and say whether she was entitled to the sum mentkaped. Mr. Acton acid his contention ,would be that. for the amount he was called upon to pai ;Mr. Winstaoley was eieitrly liable to he indemnifier( bY him. 110 left the matter entirely in his Honour's ißttilig. Mr. Browne said his contention was that the mother was not partially dependent upon Mr. Cooper. He did not make a weekly payment to enable her te oteain the necessaries of life. - - .</p>
        <p>_ His Honour said that looking at the facts, the person Who supported the mother with the daily necessaries of life was the deceased brother. The brother who had given, evidence gave the mother presents, but the contri- butions were spasmodic and only provided her extra wants. He was disposed to take the view that the deceased supplied her with. the nec,ersaries of life, and therefore he was the person upon whom she was depen- dent.. He would therefore giee•an award for the aopli- cant againet the respondent (Mr. Davenport) for £260, and costs on the highest scale. His Honour then decided to hear argument as to the question of indcmnity. Mr. Actoo said Mr. Tulle,* had admitted that Mr. Winstacley was in the position of a sub-contractor. who employed the deceaeed man at the time the aceiient happened. The object of secti..n four of the Act was universally admitted. It laid dovire that in the case of a sub-contractor, although the person injured was not employed by the undertakers. the undertakers she:mid:be primarily liable to the work Ilan, but should have a right of idemnity against the sub.contractor, who was liable undtr the Act to pay comp:nsation. Mr. Tullook in reply to M. ctnr, contendel that the sub-contractor was not liable. It was not the case, a 5 his friend haql put it, and would have his Honour believe, that his client was liable unless he was also liable as an undertaker. Hi a Tionour.Made a second award that Mr. Winstinlev should indemnify Mr. Davenport for the amount of £2604 and also costs to follow. Be gave Mr. TulloA leave to appeal. NATIONAL TOAST.—" Here's to john Bull, Pat and Sandy in Grant's Morella Cherry Brandy 1" Most delicious and comforting on a damp or frosty day. Try a 3d" nip of it and yo,i will be pleased. Sold at all bars, also in bottle by Wine Merchants Don't be put off with in erior m-Aces.—Tiros GRa.rur AND Sows, Maidstone, Makers o Cherr Whiskey, Sloe uiu &amp;c.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>11Ht-4: EXAMIN2R FRIDAY DECEMBER 15, 1999.</head>
        <p>11Ht-4: EXAMIN2R FRIDAY DECEMBER 15, 1999.</p>
        <p>OUR ALIVIANACK ILLUSTRATION. The Walker Fountain.</p>
        <p>The " Examiner " sheet', almanack for 1900 which is presented with to-day's issue is embellished with a drawing of the fountain presented to the Borough by Sir Peter Walker, Bart.. in memory of his 'grand- father Mr. Peter Walker. Tee people of Warrington' and district will, ot course, be batter able to appre- ciate the value of this aedition to the attractive- 1 'less of the Town Hall and Park when the fountain is erected. At present mush is left to the imagina- tion, but from the drawing there seems little doubt but that the fountain is to be of a most handsome and. eleburate character. In manufacturing towns there is generally little that tends to cultivate the sense of the beautiful, and Warrington is no exception in this respect. Tuere are few places in the town which may be said to appeal t ) the festhetic,but the gift of Sir Peter walker, Bart., should prove a very acceptable addition to whatever features of attrae- tiveness the town at present possesses. The fountain is classic in style, and harmonizes with the architecture of the. Town Hall, in front of which it is to be placed. Fears are entertained that the erection may, to some slight extent obscure the view of the hall, but any disadvantages of this kind will probably be fatly compensated for .by the beauty of the fountain, It will be a cast iron spray 'erection, with a ground basin 40 test in diameter, enriched by bronze portrait medallions of the donor land members of the family. On the centre portion there will be four seated figures over life size, modelled by Mr. D. W. Stevenson.,B.S•A., and repre- senting art, science, literature, and commerce which by the way will in themselves be triumphs of the irontoundera' cratt. A centre pedestal having twelve panels decorated with the Zidiacal signs, each sign being surrounded with the flora suitable to the month represented supports an upper basin. Around the rim of this ornamental basin are eight sea urchins spouting water from horns, whilst over this, four entwined dolphins carry, the terminal basin to a height 37 feet from the ground. The fountain has been designed and is being cast by Messrs. Walter Macfarlane and Company, art founders of Possil Park, Glasgow. A. dAay has taken place in the erection of the fountain but we understand that Me.srs. Macfarlane are in no way responsible for it. It is anticipated that the in- augural ceremony will be performed about March next. _ .</p>
        <p>The following is a. copy of the letter in which the offer of the gift was made :—" Ormaston Manor, Derby, Ist December, 1898. Dear Mr. Mayor, It has been brought to my notice that the gift of my late grandfather, Peter Walker, of £1.;000 for the benefit of the town of.Warrington has now been exhausted and having, by the courtesy of yourself and the Town Clerk bad an oppcgrtunity of. penn- ing the accounts relating thereto, I and other members of our family should for the sake of old and present associations, be much gratified if the town of Warrington could do us the honour of accepting a second gift of £l,OOO for the same general purposes. Will you kindly bring this matter before, the Council,and also aik their accept- ance from the same source of a further sum of £l,OOO for a fountain to be erected in the space in trout of the Town dell or any other site to be ap- proved by yourself and the council in memory of my umndfather, the late Pater Walker.-1 rema in Mr. Mayor, yours very truly. Peter Walker. To his, Worshipful the Mayor, Alderman J. E. Wrigh-, J,P., Town Hall, Warrington."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>STARLAND.</head>
        <p>STARLAND.</p>
        <p>Lecture by Archdeacon Wilson.</p>
        <p>"Some points in Stellar Astronomy " was the subject of a most inter sin and instructive lecture by the Ven Archdeacon Wilson, of Rochd tie, at the ,third meet- ing of the Warrington Literary and Philosoohical So iety held in the Co-operstive Rall, Cairo-street on Mlnd sy evening. The lecture was illustrated by lantern slides, shown on the screen by M. H. N. Houghton. To Rey, Morley Stevensm (president of the society) briefly in- trodcted the lecturer. Archdearon WlLsoe in the course of hislecturA said someone had suggested to him that it was tautwlogy to .peak of "stellar astronomy," but the word astronomy as It was ornately U99e g weruei the annemo3n.and but be was limiting iums3lt that ,</p>
        <p>about the stars, and that was uac eesil; the word "stellar." He wouei only tal number of points, and eudeavout to malto teos even to those who had not had at: upporeenity of‘ e l- ing the subject. The beautiful photograplas welfci eo of interest, and they had been placed at his dispeset oy number of friends who had ;seer oe,gaged in the seedy of astronomy. He would in the fi -at piece, explain weat was not alw .ys understood by a t • Nees, prle thought that it brought thing,. them. That was not the use of the telescope. L WAS not intended to make them nearer or magnify thsm—for no telescope bad aver shown any magnitude at all —but Simply to increase the light they get. A el do was then thrown on the screen to show tee boeutiful order of the stars, which led them to the conclusion that t ach had a connection with the other. Tnis problem al uot, how- ever. teen solved. The lectUier proceeded to comment upon the wonderful results which had been obtaiced by photography in astronomy. The number of stars,the Archdeacon pointed out, as shown by photography, was greater even than the number shown by the most powerful telescopes. They could thus study stars which had never been seen before and never would be seen by-the human eye directly. By an exposure of three and four hours the faint stars were recorded on the photographic plate and thus their knowledge of the stars was increased. Pnotn- graphs of stars were exhibited, each possessing some point of interest which was fully explained, and great interest was centred in the photograph of the Sun with its nebula taken during the eclipse c 4 1860. It was seen in Spain, and the lecturer witnessoi it in the presence of a number of astronomers who had been sent from all parts of the globe. He (the Arch deacon) went at the expense of the English Government, the Eiglieh tax- payers, but he enjoyed himself most thoroughly, all the arrangements for their comfort being of the most complete cherac.er. (Laughter.) Proceeding ha explain the distance of the stars from the : earth, Some interesting figures were given to afford the audience an idea of the enormous distance of some of the stars. They talked of millions of miles-93,000,000 or 94,000,000 et miles to the eun—hut thee did not give the best idea of the distance, and he would, therefore, put the matter in another way. If they were to take a [railway ticket to gr) round the earth, and they could go 100 miles for a penny—very cheep bye the t ye— (lauefiter)—£l only would be required to convey them 24,000 miles round the earth, £lO would be the railway fare to the moon, and £4,000 for a trip to the sun. But what would take them to the nearest fixed titer ? a sum a good deal more than the national debt, which was about £600,000,000. They would have to pay £ 200,000,000 more and even that did not include the re- torn ticket. (Leuehter and applause) Mr. R. A. Proctor's map showing the directions, in which stare were moving was next snown, and refer- ence was - afterwards made to variable stars and periodic stars. The causes leading te this interest- ing feature of •astronomy were explained and the lecturer passed on to the knowledge which had been gained of the compoaitioa of the stare by Means of the spectroscope. It had been found that some of the elelnimita of the stars were The same as those composing the earth but there were others which were unknown. At the elose,the PRESIDENT observed that the audience had been intensely interested, especially s ) bee sues the lecture had opened one's mind upon the inamease and ' difficult problems of which he ventured to say many of them had no conception befere they came to the room that evening. It showed also what an immense number of undiscovered facts that science of astronomy had brought out. (Hear, hear and applause.) • A hearty vote or thanks was accorded to Archdeacon Wilson on the proposition of the Rector aremled by the Rev. W. H. Drummond.—ln replying, the Archdeacon at the suggestion of els.. Drummond, explained why we did not see the lewd&amp; in November. Be showed how diffieult it was to calculate the exact time when the leonids would be visible from the earth owing to the disturbances of Saturn and .Tupiter which sometimes interfered with the course of the leonids and upset the best calculations. The legeride appeared between nine o'elock arid one o'clock in the daytinr) on the 15th of November. We were unfortunately on the sun side, and therefore not ss well able to Kee them. 'rho loonids appeared in the Gaited Steres about five o'cloelt in the nrnoiniy.. but there waa no S lover of leonids as w,.8 seen in 1866. The tneetine cinclud-d with the passine . of a vete of thanks to the lanternist.</p>
        <p>CADBURY'S Cocoa. on the teFtirno of the Lancet " represents the standmi of highest purity. It is fustirel, free from all foreign substances, such as kola, malt. hoes /cc., nor is alkali used to darken the colour (and so deceive the eye). Dr. Andrew Wilson in a recent article in the Illustrated Langan News writes : " Cocoa is in itself a perfect food, and requires no addition V drugs whatever CADBURY'S Cocoa is absolutely pure, and shoulebe taken Iby old awl young, at all times and in all seasons ;for ! Children it is an ideal beverage, promoting healthy growth and development in a remarkable degree. instst on having CAD BURY's, as other Cocoas are often substituted fat the sake of extra profit. Sold only in Paaksts end Tins— Advt. 3.</p>
        <p>' LINSEED COvIPOUND ' (TrAdo givas ex- Ted zation without strain, 90.. 13A. SoU by Chemists only.—Advt.</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Gospel Hall, Foster Street. On Sunday night Messrs. W. Roden and J. Green con- ducted the service, Psalm 14 and 2, Timothy 3 forming the basis of remarks, The foul bath said in his heart, "There is no God." There are several aspects of foolish- ness, a weak intellect, one who wastes his talents and his money, others play the tool bet the worst is one who is blind to his s3ul's eternal welfare,and tries to convinse himself that Creation and Nature are the result of natural causes. The reason of this folly is because they are corrupt and cannot bear the thought of standing in the presence of perfect holiness. If we submit ourselves to God's righteousness, His holiness shows us our guilt and then He cleanses that guilt by applying the precious blood of Christ and being cleansed our delight is found in His presence. The wide spread doctrine of infidelity and similar cre-di is a prof that we ar3 in the last days of this dispensation of grace. Very soon the Lord will come as promisel and remove His people from this scene and the world must then enter upon its day of judgment. Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church. Anniversary ser vices were held on Sunday at the Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church, and were well attended. The Rev. C. H. Hocken preached at the morning service on., 0 praise God in His Holiness." The pulpit at the evening service was occupied by the Rev 11. G. Roberts, who took for his subj ct "A divine fact in human nature—A Christian awl external feet in the history of the world.'' Two anteems were creditably rendered by the choir, Mr. B. Wadsworth presiding at the organ. On Monday evening, the coagregational tea party was held when a good number sat down. Afterwards the Rev T T Lambert delivered a lecture on "The sea- dogs of Plymouth." The chair was occupied by the Rev C H Hocken, who made alstatement with regard to Ithe finances of the trust fund, and said he hoped that the total amount realizsd would be about £25 —Tne Ilecturer commenced by giving an historical se ting to his remarks, and observed matt the life of E izabeth called for spec at attention. He introduced Hawkins, Drake, and Winter and detailed their exphits and their adventures with the Spaniards. their expeditions and their privateering. In cmc'usion he led up to the formation of the Spanish Arm s&amp; and its defeat. A silver collection was tak-m, and the total proceeds of the collections at the anniversary services amounted to £22 10s.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Stopford Brooke at Warrington. The Rev. Stopford A. Brooke, M.A., L.L.D., preached at Cairo-street ()hapel on Sundae morning. He took for his text the third verse of the 37th chapter of Ezekiel: "And He said unto me, 8u of men, can these bones live? And I answer(' 1, 0 Lord God Thou knowest." Tne subject was profoundly treated, and some of his picturesque phrases showed all the genius of an artist. Re pointed out that a grain of goodness either in an in- dividual or a nation was indestructible and would never perish. In the end evil must perish and be blotted out of the universe. The father of evil was covetousness, and its daughters were corruption, cruelty, violence and a host of dreadful creatures who fed upon toe vitals of the people. To slay covetousness in the heart was to slay the:n all. To let him live and continue to live was to scatter evil over the nation. When they looked over Europe they saw that covetousness was taking a harder ' grip upon the people. The cry of "more, more" went up continually to the listening earof God, and the re sult was that they lived always upon the edge of war. Nations might cry tor peace and the prayers of the seerld be effered up to the throne of Goi to win them peace and stop war, hut they would never make one step towards true peaci until they got covetousness out of the hearts of the people. It 'as not true peace that the nations of the evith desired at present. The peace they wanted had other names. It was a peace for the further development of their worldly desires ; a development of what produced jealousy, hatrei, envy, cruelty, and dissension. The result of that would ever be war. It always had been and always would be. War was .naturally the punishment of greed. Till greed stopped war would go on. If they could not stop covetousness altogether, they could prevent it gaining the mastery. It was tending towards mastery in this country. It was struggling hard for the throne of England, and was melting its influence felt not only in trade and commerce but re science, art, and literature. All classes in society seemed to be infected by a desire to get more. It was actually accepted by the majority of the people as a good aim and a good thing in life. It sounded incredible in a civilized nation, and even more in a Christian nation. If it prevailed and obtained mastery over all individuals and the Government of the people, the nation was doomed. But there was a strong hope against that dire happening to be found both in England and Europe, and it was growing steadily. A great pert of their people lay scattered and dry in the ' valley of deitruction,and he asked like the prophet of old •‘ Son of man, can these dry bones live ?" Was all ; 7 -r-lend to take the germ of death? Was there no I ri,urreztlou for this great people? Thera were false [ pr:Thete who gild" no, that things would go from bad Ito worse." Bd.!: the true prophet b Aieved that the good 1 would triumph. lie b lieved in a resurrection, and in the Ugh:. of that faith he looked abroad and saw how 1 many nen) were in the ' .ad who had not bowed their knpe,4 to lovetousnesi. i`aey could each of them do 1 some litie. fhey eetild net take the part of the rrophet; 1 that ,cc-s ti-; high fir rhe,i. But they could live the ! ~••• • = . , ' . 1- .3-7' Joni in Christ PISII3. They ' could be just towards their fellow-men as G:).1 was just to them, and help, direct, and guide them whether in Ihe Council, in Parliament on change, or in basiness. They could do all justice, and love man another. If they did that they were part of the redemption of the country In concliision he applied the text to the resurrection of the soul in the individual.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>FIRE AT WESTON.</head>
        <p>FIRE AT WESTON.</p>
        <p>A Barn Burnt to the Ground.</p>
        <p>A. barn beleneine te Mr. Thomas Ford, Cavendish Farm, Weston, centsining a large quantity of nnthrashed wheat, an 1 uethrastted sr:raw, and a farm lurry,was on Monday night buret down to the gronnd and damage to the extent of 2200 resulted. Immediately the out- break was noticed, (about 7-15), Mr. Thomas Ford, junior, rode per, haste t) Reneern for the assistance of the local Fire Brigade, and the 130 of St. John's Church waS vu19: coatinu nl4ly With the view of summoning assietance. Soon Constable W. Wylie was upon the scene, and with the aid of a number of villagers covered the adjacent haystacks with tarpaulins, and took pre- cautionary nvasures against a further spread. About eight o'clock the Runcorn Fire Brigade reached the dis- aster, but by that time the fire was complete mast3r of the situation and r se,ed fiercely. The old-f'ashioned loop. holed building looked for all the world like a large fur- nace, and when the roof cellaosed myriads of sparks flew into the sky illuminating the district for miles around. The attempts of the Fire Brigade to obtain water fr )m the mains were futile, and it was only with the ereatest difficultly that a supply was obtAined from an atitcent pit. When , the hose began ta play it W4S at once seen that the efforts of the Brigade were doomed to failure, and they had to content themselves with practically a spectators' share in the work. The bti lade remained oa the scene until the small hours of the morning, but by that time several had deserted their pests, and at one time the nozzle was unmlnned allowing the water to run to waste; though all the time, the pump-eands were work- ing as they thought to a purpose. it was net until Mr. James Tudor (manager of the Wes;on Work) took matters into his own heeds the fellowine morning that the fire was put out. Efforts at fire extinguishing formed the subject of anything but complimentary remarks. Constahles Wylie and Wilson deserve hign credit for their effortS —Superintendent D 'eaarson, of the Runcorn Police and Superintendent Et Wright, of the Fire Brigade also visited the scene of the fire.—The whole place was completely destroyed, but the damage is covered by insurance.</p>
      </div>
