      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>DEFECTS IN OUR EDUCATIONAL</head>
        <p>DEFECTS IN OUR EDUCATIONAL</p>
        <p>SYSTEM.</p>
        <p>Dr. Macnamara at Warrington.</p>
        <p>" A. neglected side of imperialism" was the title of an address delivered in Bold-street schoolroom on Tuesday evening by Dr. Macnamara. the editor of the "School- master," and one of the leading, men connected with the Natioaal Union of Teach3rs. The meeting was held under the anspices vf the Warrington and District Teachers' Association, and a large number of the mein- b rs were present, but there was only a small attendance of tie general public. The Mayor (Alderman Roberts) presided, and amongst those present were Councillors Shaw Green, Greening, and Dr. Gernall. Mr, Upson announced that apologies for non-attendance had been received from Mr. Finney and Mr. Stansfield. The MaYois in the course of his address said he was very glad to have the opportunity of presiding at a meeting to introduce for the first time Dr Macnamara to a Warrington audience. Be occupied a very important position in the world_of education, and was doing good service in a public capacity as a, member of the London School Board. (Hear, hear.) He.(the Mayor) did not Profess to have any special acquaintance with the sub- ject the lecturer would deal with, but as an ordinary- citizen he held the opinion that money spent in the cause of education was, perhaps, likely to procure the best return almost of any public money expended many direction. (Hear, hear) The old-fashioned idea that anything would do in the way of education was passing away and we were beginning to realise that if the battles of the future were to be won by this country they must be won by educated Englishmen and English- women. (Hear, hear.) He u=deretoed that Dr. Macaa- mara would speak mere of elementary than secondary or technical education. but he should like to take the opoortuniv of saying that it was quite time that Britishers realized the enormous competition which they had now te face as a ina,nufacruring nation—a position which did not exist 20 or 30 years ago—be- cause of the advanced position the artizan classes on the Continent oicupied in regard to the question of second- ary and technical eckcation as compared with their own nation. (Hear, hear.) If what Dr. Mactiamara had to tell them would stimulate the interest of the general public in regard to theirschools, and of the teachers tied scholars, he Would be doing good service.. He (the Mayor) was not only glad to have an opportunity of meeting Dr. Macnamara, but of meeting the members of a profession whom he held in such high esteem. He was an old pupil of an old elementary schoolmaster, and he should never feel that he had said too much about the debt which he owed to that master and his _staff in the days when he was a lad at school. (Hear, hear.) Anything the lecturer said which might suggest practi. cal application on the part of the Corporation in regard to this very important qeestion would, at any rate so far as it was in his (the Mayor's) power to facilitate it, have the careful cansideration of the authorities and such assistance as they could properly reader. (Ap- plause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>DR. MACNAMARA'S ADDRESS.</head>
        <p>DR. MACNAMARA'S ADDRESS.</p>
        <p>Dr. M&amp;CNAMARA., at the outset explainel what he meant by the word " imperialism " which he had used in the title of his address. He said that true imperial- ism meant that a nation should be happy, contented, nd should be living up to the ideals of the hiahest forms of citizenship, should have secured freedom and .justice for all under its E,rovernment, whether at home or abroad. He recognised that they could not build up a great nation by waving the National flag. They hod to develop the national character and intellect so that their people would be able to inset the great struggle before them. Allusion was made to the great heritage of the children of this empire and to the important work before them. It was necessary that they should be well edu- cated. M any of them mivel opportunities thrauv,h the fault of their parents. When toe working class realized their proper obligations towards their children it would be better.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>IRREGULAU SCUOOL ATTENDANCR,</head>
        <p>IRREGULAU SCUOOL ATTENDANCR,</p>
        <p>The million who were nearly always the same every time, and the public money spent upon them was prac- ticallr wasted. He was not fanatic in this matter. He did not ask for an absolutely perfect regularity, but he said it was a gross scandal to all concerned in this great question that it should be true that practically one-fifth of the children, whose names should be on the rolls Were always away, and three-quarters of a million were never there at all. Public (-pinion must be more robust on this question. People did not care Magistrates were not prepared very often to punish parents for the neelect of their children. They suffered from too much liberty on the subject. That was what was the matter with them. Some of those present had half had their chance, but these children were in the making. Their time was before them, and on their behalf he pleaded because they needed the schools most of all. In Warrington there were 12,400 on the rolls of the elementary schools If he went into the schools and counted the children on an average day he would find 9,900 present, or 2,500 absent either morning or afternoon. Allowing a generous estimate for sickness and destitu- tion, he ventured to say the result was bad. The parents were largely to blame in the matter. The great bulk of parents in Warrington, and indeed all over the country. endeaVoured to send their children to school, and made great sacrifices to do so, but there was a small section of parents who, if they were less thought- less, might send tkrir children with greater ramlarity. t lvs town the average child stayed away a whole day every week. If they waited until the parent who per- sistently kept her child away from school develoned the right sort of conscience they would wait a long time. The only thing for them was compulsion, and nothing short of compulsion would develope a conscience. Their 80 per cent. fell very sadly b dow the position of the average town of their description There were 63 county boroughs If they took a list of those boroughs and put Warring- ton in its position il, wou'd stand 50th. They did not even come to the level of the ad ninistrative counties. There were 50 administraeivi counties. Of those War- rington was 45th. Could they do more than they were doing at present? The School Attendance Committee no doubt did all it could.</p>
        <p>MORE SCHOOL ATTENDANCE _OFFICERS REQUIRED</p>
        <p>They ought at least to have half-a-dowse Shoot Attend- ance officers. He did not know how many they had, but they ought to have half-a-dozen. (A Voice: "Three.") It would pay them to have more. If they raised the nercentaee from 80 to 81 the increase in their grant Would be £2OO. If they reached the excellence of Harley, 89 per cent., the grant would be increased to £l,BOO, or 21 in the £on the heal rates. If they reached the percentage of Sunderlaed, 87, it would be ineroasscl to £1,400; or Hull and Liverpool, 86, to £1,200. They had therefore a financial interest in the matter, though after all the financial interest was the smallest, because there was the welfari of the children to consider. The-teachers also had to do more. They had to turn themselves into honorary attendance officers. (Etear, hear.) The main fault lay with the parents who were indifferent and careless. They ought to be honest with them, tell them the brutal troth, and make them understand that for the sake of the country and the child he tJught to be at school. As an impartial observer he could not call the echool attendance in this town as satisfactory as one woull desire to 1330 it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>PARENTS FINED TOO LENIENTLY.</head>
        <p>PARENTS FINED TOO LENIENTLY.</p>
        <p>The average fine imposed on p +rents summoned for Fenling their children irregularly to school was is. S&amp;L a case at Warrington while in Glasgow it was 103. As a result Glasgow was far and away ahead of them in the matter of attendance. Why should the fine be h.avier ? _ _</p>
        <p>_ Because it wa4 prect'c illy the same people who were summoned time after time, and heavier fines were Lound to effect an improvement. As it was they could afford to pay the small fine and keep the children from schnol. They brought the law into disrepute. Let them rscognize the fact that there was no' hing for it but cam- pulion for a certain number of parents. CHILDREN LEFT SCHOOL TOO EARLY.</p>
        <p>Far 1898-t;-e—ic-n-e-or Ateenda-n-ce Committee returned 12,057 on the rolls. He observed that of these 3,714 were ten yeirs of age and upwards. That was 30 per cent. Throughout the country at the present moment the percentage of children over the age of ten was 35. For 1898 in Sunderland, the percentage wes 43. Accord- ing to their return in Warrington far 1898 they had in standards four, five, six and seven —the very standards which rendered their exneaditure on education perma- nent and valuable-2,496 children, or 20 per cent. They were putting their money into bags with holes in ttern. In Barrow-in-Ferness the percentage wss 30. In Brad- ford it was 27, and in Exeter, Oldham, Halifax and other towns, 25. The Mayer had referred to the develop- ment of secondary education. They could not build first class secondary education moon the sort of founds.- ' tien to which he had referred. The average child stayed away one day out of five. and the great balk of the children left school at to early an age. The foundation must bP made more thor sugh before successful second try eaucation coul Ihe introduced. It was from standard four upw., rim that the rd 'cation of the ethid was of per- maneut value. If that foundation were not laid secon- dary or technical education would be fruitless. This again was smatter for the parents. The temptation to sead the child to work early was a very great one, but ' the longer the child stayed at school the better it was for the operatives. There were plenty of six foot labourers unemployed because of the erten,: to which the marllel was filled by juvenile lahour. It was dieastrous to the child also, because the longer the child staved at the school the better chance he would hay:. in the future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>CONTINUATION EICEIO')LS A_NUSNO DI E Ft</head>
        <p>CONTINUATION EICEIO')LS A_NUSNO DI E Ft</p>
        <p>They had continuation schools in Warrington ; but they were not cruitinu‘tion Toev were schaols in which they did over again what tip.: did in the day sch3ol, The term was a mianowr. Tie education of the early days was tint pertnanenc.and they were spend- iog money to do the same thing over atin. The money</p>
        <p>they were spending on technical instruction and muni- cipal classes would be wasted unless they did something to stimulate the public conscience and inske people understand tbe gravity of the issue. The money they received for education from the Central Exchequer and the special aid grant was not sufficient, and they had to rely for the remainder upon voluntary assistanee which he deprecated, Tne whole of the people should bear the expense.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>EVERY WOMAN</head>
        <p>EVERY WOMAN</p>
        <p>Should fend two stamps for our 32 pave Itluitrated Book containing valuable information of how all Irrogularities and Obstructions may be entirely avoided or ramoved by simple means. Recommended by eminent Physicians the only Safe, Sure and Genuine Remedy. Never .alts. Thousand. 3 of genuine unlieited Testimlnials from all over the World.—P. LtNcamtp, Forest-read, Dalston, London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>SUGGESTIONS.</head>
        <p>SUGGESTIONS.</p>
        <p>lie suggestorl that the Council should make a levy and dispense the money. lie was not advocating a School Board or seeking to intlrfere with the clergy. They could do very well without a School B )ard. Ile farther suggested that ie each Beard of Management the Council should have a nomiaee. Out of the rates 63. in the £ might be &amp;A asile fnr the local suppon of the schools. That would realize £5.460. Speaking of the number of ex-pupil *teachers, pup,l teachers, and Article 68 teachers, compared with certificated teachers in Warrington, he sai,l it contrasted very unfavourably with London. He contended that the pupil teachers were not given a fair chance, and it was little wonder that so many were unable to pass the scholarship examination when the time they should be occupied in studying was given up to teaching. Pupil teachers were put to journeyman work before tany were ready and they were therefore ueable to follow up their studies . and very often failed to get into college. This was done in order to save money. There seemed to be a public disregard of how schools were managed. No man was a true patriot or worthy of the name of imperialist wtio did not see that the governing forces were changing; and that there was a decline of physical- ism in favour of intellectualism. Ile might have put his case before them somewhat severely but it was not • worth his while to come tlvre and talk platitudest. (Applause.) • n.-wre _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>VOTES OF MANES.</head>
        <p>VOTES OF MANES.</p>
        <p>Councillor SHILLCOCIC in proposing a vote of thanks to Dr. Macnamara said he did not think the lecturer knew all the diffilulties they tali to contend with in Warrins:ton in regard to school attendance. They- sufferei greatly through thefl migratory tendencies of people who canal to Warriogtoa for a short time and then departed for all parts. Theo they had much in-: fantile sickness to cootend with which tended to decrease the attendance. He thought they might fairly con- gratulate themselves upon the attendance in Warrington and probably the amendment to the Act which came bate force in January next would make an improve- ment. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Everrs seconded. and the MAYOR aupported. The resolution wss unanimously carried. Dr. NIACNAMMIA. responded and propose] a vote of thanks to the Mayor for presidina. M. llPsou seconded,and the vote was cordially agreed to. The meeting then terminAted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>TRADES UNIONISM.</head>
        <p>TRADES UNIONISM.</p>
        <p>Meeting of the Warrington Trades I and Labour Council. I The monthly meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Coancil was held at the " Waggon and Horses " Inn, Butterrnarket-street, on Tuesday evening, Mr. A. C. Binks. the President...presiding over a large attendance. PROPOSED ATTENDANCE AT CHURCH. The CHAIRMAN thought it would bring trades unionists closer together if the members of the Council together with the members of the societies which the Council re- presented accompanied the President to church once a year. He took that opportunity of inviting them to accompany him as President to St. Paul's Church on any Sunday that the Council might deem fit. He had inter- viewed the Vicer of St. Paul's and heliaid he would be most happy to address the Couecil on any Sunday after- noon that they might arrange if it was convenient to him. He (the speaker) theught that the hest day wcruld he either the last Suhday in the old year, or the first Sunday in the new year. He thought it would ne no disgrace to attend divine service once a year. (Hear, hear.) Mr. GRIMLY, strongly disegreLd with the proposal. He did not think religion was any good to thorn. Mr. MARSHY thought it would be a grand.. thing to attend church. Other secie:ies did so and why should not they ? Mr. GRRALY was of the opinion that ministers did not know anything of their grievances. Mr. C. E. BROWN was in favour of the proposal. He thought it would be a goo I thing for them to attend Divine service and move! the following resalution : "That we recognise tae first Sunday in May as' Labour Day,' and accompany the President to Divine service." Mr. WiLeacit seconded. e Mr. STorr proposed an amendment to the effect that the first Suniay in May be recognised as "Labour Day," not necessarily by accompanying the President to church. Mr. BATTERSBY thought it would be unwise to .pass a definite resoletion of the nature of the one before them, except the minbers of the diffsrent societies were in Accordance with it. An addition was made to both the resolution and the amendment to the effect that they would be carried out subject to the approval of the societies represented. After discussion al vote was taken and ten voted for the resolution and nine against. The CHAIRMAN was dissatisfied at there being so small a majority and a.ked permission to withdraw his sug- gestion, Mr. Brown would not withdraw his resolution and it was decided to vote again. Eighteen voted for the resolution and two against. The matter then dropped. CONSCRIPTION—IN VERESTING LETTER. The SECRETARY read toe following letter which he had received from the Lincoln Trades and Lebo ar Council, " Dear Sir.—at a meeing of the above council the fol- lowing letter was read fronts one of the delegates : "Fellow Members, I beeeve our sympathy is with the soldiers of the Eturtisn and Transvaal armies, who have teen ordered to settle a grievance by killing one another. I wonder if we have thought that it nrght be our lot to be forced into like conflic's, unleos be agitation, we waken the workers up to the real meaning of the Sscre- trary for War's statement (I irliave male in the Houss of Lords) viz. :—" That at so:ne future date he would introduce a Bill to cosnoot each couatv to raise so many men for the Army." Surely this means Coeslription What steps shall we take ? Can we be Trade Unionists and Conscripts? Can the Council suggest some action to resist this attempt at Conscription ?"—The Council decided to ley tnis question before the Ir tdes' Councils of the United Kingdom, asking them for an expression of. opinion on the question of " Conscription," how it will affect the workers, and whether it %mild be possihle to organise a united resistance, should this or any future Government try to force " Conscription" upon this country. Should your Council pass any res dation, or make any suggestion, ws amid bs glad of a copy for future reference. On behalf of the Council. H. Ducx- WeRTIS (president), H. M. LANs (secretary)." ; It was resolved that the letter lie on the tabie till nest ; meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>ACCOUNTS.</head>
        <p>THE ACCOUNTS.</p>
        <p>Tian balance shest sabithted and showed a btl- awe in hand of 54 43.</p>
        <p>It was unanimously adopted THE FILESMITH'S STRIKE—DEIERMINATION</p>
        <p>OF THE MEN.</p>
        <p>Mr. Barman; secret try of the Filesmiths' Associa- tion, said that the strike was unfortunately progressing. The masters seemed determined to be stubborn, and the men were equally determined to go on—(hear; hear)— and there were no signs of them giving way. They were : as firm as a rock, and were very well supported by trades' societies. He took that opportunity of thanking most heartify those societies who helped them. He hoped that if the strike should be further prolonged. they would again render assistance, as it was now nearing a crucial point. They had been out twenty-five weeks, which was a bag time for such a small boiy of men to fight. He was proud to say that the men were now fighting with the same determination as they did the first moment they were out. A good deal of the Rupport was due to the energy of the hard vror, ing committee which they had formed. They never expected that the struggle would be so protracted, and he believed the masters would be labouring under the same impression. It amused almost impossible that the strike would last such a long time, but there did n3t seem the slightest signs of giving way on either side. It was a matter of who should lalld out the lorLres!,and so long as tra les'unionists rallied by their side there was not the slightest dount in his mind as to who would be the ultimate victors.</p>
        <p>Tho CIL&amp;IOW,/ naivA a r-s)lution to the effect that the Colwell continue their subport of the tileamithe during their prolonged di4lut. an I (11 all in its power to help and encauraga the nvn ia their eadeavour to get a living wage. _ _ .</p>
        <p>MC WILCOCK se.c)nded and the illatiOn vi•l3 earriel unanimously. The mitter of holdioz a.noz.her public me,tine as clusiderei and it; Wai tl Lve i t the Execu- tive COMMittIP.</p>
        <p>Ittr. BATTER9BY having' thank 'd the tnemblr9 f sing the r s )lutions the orec-eninize terminated.</p>
