      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>TilE EXA MIN ELL</head>
        <p>TilE EXA MIN ELL</p>
        <p>DE-JEWBER 15ra 1333</p>
        <p>THE relief of Kimberley is Methuen's Tough delayed.a Lard Ar itLETHUEN Job.</p>
        <p>has found the Boers in another strong position at Magersfontein, a little to the eastward of Spytfontein and the railway line. The position consists of a series of hills favourable to entrenchments and to the mounting of heavy guns. Directly after the withdrawal from the Modder River the Boers fell back eastward, and then they made a detour to reach Magersfon- tein, where they commenced to make trenches sheltered from artillery fire, assisted by the Ger- man officers whose skill has more than once been shewn in the construction of defensive works on the hills which abound on the threefold line of our advance. Not until the Orange Free State can be invaded will there be any table-land offering ad- vantages of which our Generals will be able to make use. The kekkings of the Boers from parts of Ladysmith are now intelligible. A portion of the investing force has been drawn off and, with reinforcements from the Free State, a consider- able army has • been gathered together to make a final stand to prevent the relief of Kimberley. It was in endeavouring to drive out the Boers from their strong position at Magers- fontein that Lord METHUEN has received a check. On Saturday he commenced operations by a vigor- ous artillery fire intended to enfilade the position and to make it untenable. The Boers had an ex- perience of the effect of Lyddito shells, and yet they clung to the position with a tenacity which compells our admiration, and opens our eyes to the elaborate training they must have received in this new kind of defence under the tuition of the foreign officers whom they have recruited from all parts of Europe to aid them in the war for which they have been so manifestly preparing. Oa Mon- day the artillery fire was renewed, and the Boer guns were temporarily silenced, possibly because the gunners received instructions to expose them- selves no further. At the proper moment our in- fantry, covered by continued artillery fire advanced to storm the position, and then the were met with a deadly rifle fire from the trenches. The Boers obstinately held their own, and our men had to retire. The lii?;liland regiments were entrusted with the duty of leading the storming line, and they seem to have suffered more than the other troops engaged. The official account of the battle does not materially differ from the first account received, but it states that the attack on the south side was the main attack, and that it failed. The long high kopje was still held by the enemy, estimated to be at least 12,000 strong. Our loss has been great and included General WAUCHOPE an able and popular officer. Lord METHUEN adds that he holds his position, and is entrenching himself. The Boers claim to have taken forty-one prisoners, and probably the account is correct. The battle resembles the Mad- der River fight in many respects, and Lord METHEUN should be in a position to derive some advantage from his fighting, if he can keep open his communications and obtain much-needed rein- forcements. The Boers are not likely to make a direct attack on our force, and the fear is about the railway, which may be in Lord METHUE 's rear. Possibly he may be in a position to renew combat, ana with a more satisfactory result in the course of the next few days.</p>
        <p>WE are delighted to see Corn m on-sense that there is a tendency , Temperance Reform. on the part of nearly all temperance reformers to fail into line on the basis of Viscount PEEL'S report. There is now good hope of something substantial being accomplished, instead of that "for ever climbing up the climb- ing wave," which has been so depressing in its effect upon average minds, and has discouraged the reformers whose convictions are deficient in strength and tenacity. No one who has had much to do with contested elections can be ignorant of what has happened when the Church of England Temperance Society has broken off from other temperance bodies at the last moment, and proved the cause of embarrassment instead of a source of strength. Elections have been lost and the ad- vance of reform has been hindered. From the temper and resolutions of the meeting at Man- chester on Monday it may be inferred that all but the extreme sections are now united on the basis of Viscount PEEL'S report. The fact must have a potent influenee on the country and the Government. The country desires to see many changes made in our Ii censing system, and it is not disposed to wait for the con- venience of party wire-pullers and the warm sup- port of the vested interests. It desires common- sense reforms,.at the earliest possible moment. If anything coulil impress the Government it will be this unanimity and greater zeal, this combina- tion between Conservatives and Liberals in favour of putting an end to the present arrestment and indifference. It is quite possible that the Govern- ment, which thinks more of the retention of its majority than some of the higher interests of the country, may be unwilling to move, reluctant to take up a difficult question, and prepared to push it off until after the next Gone ral Election. It is equally probable that there are many persons who would prefer to see the Licensing question handled by a Government not Liberal, and not to be sus- pected of an eagerness to harass and disestablish. The problem is how to force the Government to act, or how to compel it to give room for the private efforts of zealous reformers. The first point is already gained in the consolidation of several schools of reform, and we hope that when Parliament meets such a vigorous front will be manifest as will force the Government to see that it cannot maintain a hostile attitude without loss of influence and the abandonment of its claim to be a Government of social reform. The task may be difficult, but concerted measures should be taken and an attack made at the earliest possible moment, probably by a comprehensive and well- drawn amendment to the Address. But there must be agreement beforehand, there must be no hanging back, and small details most be tempor- arily put aside in order to obtain success and to make the move the more emphatic. We believe it would be possible to carry an amendment, or at any rate, to muster such strong support for it as would break down the present disinclination to meddle with the subject. It would be a disgrace to us as a nation if, after having spent so much time and money over the Licensing Commissicn , we were to allow its principal suggestions to be thrust aside in the interests of any political party. Reform can best be obtained by the union of parties, and it is only by means of this union that the Government can be induced to act at once.</p>
        <p>The publication of an edition de luxe of Mark Twain's works reminds us that, much as we may criticise him, he stands alone as a humorist. No- body would go to him for exact information, or general history. Grant Allen's "European Gui is far preferable to "A Tramp Abroad" in that spect ; but the men who can strike off the bib the shams, and eccentricities of their time are v few, and satire so often degenerates into cold, - pleasant cynicism. Mark Twain is always amusing, and we want writers who can at once amuse and strike at follies. In fiction in England the humor- ists are very few. Even Mr. Jerome K. Jerome's shallow stream has run dry for the last year or so. We have our heroic war tales, we have a plentiful use of the deathbed in scenes of pathos, we have tales of the mean streets, and exaggerations of the follies of society, but so very little in the way of comedy in binding. There was great reason to hope that Mr. George Gissing, the acknowledged disciple of Dickens, would have helped us in that matter. His early tales were de- pressing in their realism, though wonderful in their power; but his Town Traveller marked a new vein —of facetiousness." "The Crown of Life" has not quite fulfilled the expectations to which this book gave birth. Two of the younger generation, Mr. Pett Ridge and Mr. Edward Pugh have given us some bright, short tales out of the common run, and have scored a partial success with their early novels. Mr. Zangwill has displayed an undeniable sense of humour, but it is of the saturnine kind, and he lingers over tragedy. He has not in recent years done anything in a lighter vein. Literature and drama are just now suffering in exactly oppo- site—a drama because it has too much force, the book world because it is too serious.</p>
        <p>ACCORDING to a well-informed correspondent of the Paris Figaro, the serious illness of Tolstoi is privately attributed by his Russian physicians to his persistence in a vegetarian diet. As was the case with M Sarcey, the famous French dramatic critic, who has so recently died, his malady has been diagnosed to be an enlarged liver, and the fruits of the earth are supposed unkindly to have minis- tered to its development. That may be a falla- cious theory of doctors who, as a class appear to be but slightly acquainted with the effects of different kinds of nourishment or too timid, gener- ally speaking, to be plain on the subject. Curi- ously enough, however, nr4t long ago medical opinion was to the effect that another great thinker, Mr. G. Bernard Shaw, would be unable to heal some fractured tendons in his leg' because he adhered to a vegetarian diet, yet to-day his steps are strong, and he has not departed from his faith. Tolstoi has reached a full old age, and he may well be in- clined to keep to a system which has served him for a decade or more, especially as this length of years is arrived at,notwithstanding dissipations in youth, to which he has so frequently confessed. This social reformer has by habit come to dislike meat just as much as the majority of people find it also by habit their main source of nourishment. The moral for the common-sense man is a mixed diet, of which he can vary the proportions as his condition may suggest without disturbing his mind. We in Great Britain are too much addicted to the flesh pots; no other Europeon nation shews such an average per individual.</p>
        <p>The news from the Transvaal has been of a de- pressing nature this week, and unless something happens shortly to restore our confidence in the prestige of British arms,Christmas in this country will be lacking in seasonable cheerfulness, while to many a thousand familie3 it will be a period of mourning and distress. It is inexpressibly sad- dening to read of,the terrible slaughter of man by his brother man,and the anguish is all the keener when it comes home to us through the agency of British losses. Notwithstanding the splendid, and unrivalled gallantry of our troops, they are unable to win the successes which tradition leads us to expect. Our foes are formidable fighters—per- haps as formidable as any other troops in the world—and it is being sorrowfully impressed upon us that England's triumph is to be dearly bought, though we do not for a moment believe that the price will "stagger humanity,"as Kruger modestly says. This week has been especially terrible for this country. General Gatacre and Lord Methuen have lost about 1,500 men between them, the lat ter's force being also deprive.' by death in action of</p>
        <p>THF, EXAMINER. FRIDAY, DECEMBER 15, 1899.</p>
        <p>some of its bost leaders. Great sorrow is felt everywhere at the death of General Wauchope, a most able officer and popular man. The Highland regiments have suffered heavily, and we sy mpathise warmly with our sister country in her severe losses. All hope is now centred in the Commander- in-Chief, General Buller, and wild rumours are already afloat attributing to him a decisive victory. Let us hope that rumour for once is correct.</p>
        <p>Once more is the Ship Canal Company in dis- grace locally, and likely to get a severe rapping of the keuckles. The swing-bridges have been pre- suming on the forbearance of the public and the public has risen in its wrath; or at any rate Council- lor Pierpoint has. He brought a grave indictment against the Latchford Swing Bridge, which has been leaving yawning gaps between Laneashire and Cheshire for twenty minutes at a time. This is more than a joke,and excuses re fog and dilatory steamers will not pacify the irate traveller who stands in Cheshire and watches his train or omni- bus across the water start complacently without him. We are glad that the Warrington Council has decided to take this very real grievance up,for slack- ness in attending to such matters would certainly be followed by flagrant disregard of the law and the rights of the travelling public.</p>
        <p>By the way, lest any alarm should be occasioned by the occasional daily and nocturnal groanings and shriekings which emanate from the neigh- bourhood of Stockton Heath we would state that the Canal Company's dredger has arrived "of the port "and is responsible for the pandemonium. It seems to find a difficulty in gouging out the bed of the waterway and emits those awful noises which make one recall Dante's Inferno,and similarly cheerful mental pictures. We fear it will come to a sad and sudden end at the hands of some re- vengeful" Heathen" unless it moderates its up- roar.</p>
