      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>ILLNESS OF MR. W. B. FRITH.</p>
        <p>We are gratified to learn that Mr. W B. Frith, on of Mr, J. B. Frith, Runcorn, isnow recoveriag rapidly from his recent severe illness.</p>
        <p>A DOCTOR'S KIND Ora.</p>
        <p>We are informed that Mr.Marshall,the Hon. Secretary to the Soldiers' Relief Fund, has received a letter from Dr. Ormsby, offeOng his professional services, free of charge to the wives and families o t the Reservists who have been called out in this district. HOLIDAYS AT THE RUNCORN DOCKS.</p>
        <p>Notice is published to-day that the Manchester Ship Canal Company will suspeni work at Runcorn Docks and Bridgwater Wharves on Christmas Day, Boxing day, and New Years' Day. Craft will however be able to pass through the canal and docks as usual. TEA PARTY AND CONCERT AT WESTON.</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the Runcorn Co-operative awl Provident Society, Limited, a tea party and concert toek place at the Wesleyan Schools, Weston, last Monday evening. The artistes at the latter were Miss Temper- ley, Messrs H Gree (Liverpool). Nesbitt, &amp;lagler, War. rail, and Rutter, whilst Mr W Grounds acted as accom- panist. During the interval an address was delivered by Mr. J. Scragg RUNCORN ORCHESTRAL SOCIETY CONCERT.</p>
        <p>Ihe Runcorn Orchestral Society gave a most succesa- ful concert at the Drill Hall, on Wednesday eveniag in aid of the Soldier's Relief Fund, at which there was a large audience. In addition to the contributions by the orchestra (conductor Mr. W. H. Hadley), Mr. Harry Bamber, and Mr. Bayard Haddock contributed vocal items ; Mr. Tom Knight a concert solo; and Mr. Cyril Chantler several recitations including the "Absent- minded Beggar." The accompanist was Mr. W. Cooke.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>MERSEY MISSION CONCERT.</head>
        <p>MERSEY MISSION CONCERT.</p>
        <p>Miss Bowyer and Miss Coward provided the concert party at the weekly gathering of the sailors and boat- men at the above Institute last Tuesiay, when an excel- lent programme of vocal music was followed by a very humorous sketch entitled "Blundering Barney." The items were as follows :—song, " Queen of the Earth," Mr Temple ; song, "Summer of Love," Miss Hill; song and chorus, " The ole black oss," Master Willie Shaw ; song, "Scarlet and Blue," Master J Rutter ; song and chorus, "Gipsy Jane," Miss Amy Coward; song (humorous), Mr T Wood ; song and chorus, "Soldiers of the Qu,?en," Master F Outhwaite ; song, "You're so good Daddy," Miss L Shaw ; duet, "Very Suspicious," Miss Coward and Mr Temple; song and chorus, "Shoulder to Shoulder," Mister L Tonks song, " To- morrow," Miss Coward ; song, 'I Mother's Darling," Master J Rutter ; duet," I don't want to play in your yard," Misses A Coward and L Shaw ; song,Mr T Wood. Humorous sketch," Blundering Barney," characters : Biddy, Miss Douglas ; Blundering Barney, Mr T Wake- field ; Mr Snarl, Mr Llewellyn Marsh ; quartette, "Good- night Beloved," Misses Withington, 3owrd, and Messrs Temple and Hopwood. The efficient acComoanist was Miss Garvie. The proceedings concluded with the usual votes of thanks.</p>
        <p>val. Then they came to the movement of Cromwell and his ironsides,a movement for the carrying out of reforms which had not been carried out in the previous reign. It wss an attempt ot at least a certain portion of th. uatLn zealous for religion, zealous for reform, zealeus for purity of life which they had been bent upon for many years past. Scarcely was 0 iver dead when there were plots—there had been plots in his lifetime—to revive the House of :Stewart, and to restore Catholicism in the land as the recognised religion of the people. There was then another swing in the pendulum, and they came to the revolution and introduction of William 111. The speaker went on to refer to the long period in the 18th century in which,tie said, the country went back to the kind of indifferent state in which it was not concerned much in religion at ail, and thence he proceeded to speak of the revival under" Wesley. Tha revival seemed to give promise that they Were at last to have a time of thorough reformation life in England anioagst the whole of ttic churches. Then they caine to that modern movement of the development of natural science and the doctrine of evolution, that on one side seemed to change men's thoughts about the creation of the world, about the Providence of God, and about the things that seemed to underlie most deeply the religious mind. On the other hand there was the revival of the scientific spirit in the treatment of history. The lecturer referred to John Henry Newman and the Oxford move- ment, When Newman trembled for religion, he began to think of how it was possible t s restore the authority of religion in the English nation in such a way that it could meet these changes and no disorder attend her religious life. His problem was a magnificent problem,it was rightly conceived. He was great in the way in which he apprehended it, and in apprehending it with such far seeing discernment. He was absolutely wrong in the way in which he strove to meet the difficulty. When he unlit the restoration of the authority of re- ligion in England, he thought of the restoration of the authority of his own church, and he too much mixed up religion and church as though they were one and the same thing. Religion was corporate within all the churches put together, and did not depend upon the particular authority of a particular church. All this in- dicated to them that this problem of the Church Crisis was a problem that had CO/119 to them through long and deep movements in their history. There were many of them who grew up partly under the influence of the Hign Church movement introduced by John Henry Newman, and they used to think of it a good deal as though it were a matter of ritual and incense. High Church people were regarded as a people who were interested in beautiful music. Amongst the Church of England there were, at any rate a people who introduced a larger use of hymns,anthems, and other characteristics of music into their churches. They instituted surpliced choirs and had processional hymns. And whilst there was a great deal of talk about it, and it created a great deal of stir, and many peeple thought they were departing from the simplicities of relig- ion, it did not seem as there was verymuch to make ado about. Certain Archbishops by their conduct would lead them really to believe it was all a matter about the ceremonial Use of incense. The Federation of Freo Church Councils with whom he was connected and their ceuncil was affiliated, passed a resolution in which it was declared that this was only the most trivial treatment of a most grave topic. What they had before them was really a re-crudescence, a growing again. of priestism. Referring to Walsh's " Secret History of the Oxford Movement." the lecturer said if they wished to be in a position of judging on this question, he lewdly thought they could afford to leave that book unread. But he did not think the author was able generously enough to in- terpret Newman. tie (the lecturer), thought that what the book said was substantially true. The Society of the Holy Cross consisted of Church of England ministers who were banded together for the purpose of securing the adoption in the Ceurch of Eigland of Sacramen- tal confession. Waist did they mean by that? Whatever the rubric might say they meant that confes- sion was the means recognised as a proper sacrament whereby the soul was rightly to receive absolution from the priest they wished to reintroduce the confessional in the Romanist sense. The Church Prayer Book saidl anyone might seek out his minister and take counci from him. That was a very different thing to bringing in the confessional. Toe speaker went on to refer to the society of corporate re-union which was seeking the union of Anglican Church, with the Church of Rome ; Confraternity of the Blessed Sacrament which sought to introduce the Roman Mass. In this connection the lecturer said he saw no dis- tinction between what the High Church party called the Eucharistic Sacrifice and the Mass of the Rernan Church. The whole topic as to whether there should be fasting Communion or not was associated with the Roman doctrine of the Mass. The men who en- couraged these doctrines only remained in the Church of England to more effectively do this work, which they believed had been given to them by God to do, viz., to convert the Church of England to the Church of Rome, and bring her over bodily. There was dishonesty in that kind of attitude. Their free Church solution of this crisis was net any surreoder of their principle of toler- ance, It has become too sacred to them that they should even in a little kind of panic anout the restoration of priestism in this land forsake the principle by which they had held their position. No. They wanted to use no arm of power that might be raised in order that they might in the slightest way interfere with the rights of the Romanist, Be should be allowed to follow his own conscierece. And if they would not interfere with the Romanist, they did not wish to interfere with the High Churchman following the bent of his own conscience. They bad no objection to a man following his own conscience, but they did not wish to be parties through him or anyone else of Roman- , ising the nation. If it was to be Romanized once more and handed over to the Priest, it was not to be handed over with their consent. But they were tolerant. There I was room for every man in this country to follow the bent of his own conscience, as long as he was doing that on his own eccount, and doing it at his own cost, and note compromising others who had a different con cience on the matter. There was what might be called a low Church solution, a high Church solution, and a broad church solution of this question. By their low church siltation they meant that they respected the righ ts ot the Christian laity. The low church had for the most part been understood to be the popular Church in England. It had been underatocd to be the church which was most led to use the power of laity in England, and in so tar as they advocated the rights of the laity it seemed to him that they ought to carry the low church with them. They recognized tne responsibility of every Christian man and woman to be the means whereby according to his or her own capacity and epoertunity the Gospel of the Lord Jesus Christ was preached. He (the speaker) did not recogniz. that Jesus Christ ever appointed an order of men upon whom was laid this responsibility. What he lett to be done He left to His Church, to those who should be gathered into the fold of His name. Thrir Hish church friends had somehow or other COMA to what they themselves—he was speaking now as a Congregationa- list—had greatly insisted upon viz., the independence of the church in its relation te the State ? Their High Church friends recognized that it was contrary to the sacred character of the church that it should be a kind of instrument of the State, that the church should be recognized as a kind of way in which the Sate was dis- charging its religious functions or exercising the religious side of its life. Lord Halifax consented that they should have their independence; they a!eo con- tended for the same principle. They else represented the broad church solution of this question. They were</p>
