        <p>desirous of real re-union. Thoy were anxious that those who represented ths name of Jesus Christ, of whatsoever order, should have every hindrance removed from their thorough co-opera:ion one with another. If their solution was not ore that commended itself to their brethren they could reject it. When they were dealing with an es:ablished church in this conntry that c.rried with it all the prestige of the state, which had also the national name associated with it and was supported by National endowments,then it seemed to him they had even a civil right to interfere in this matter. Their interest was simply that of Englishmen woo were concerned for the true well-being of tise nation. Tney were profoundly interested in tile true well-b2ing of the people from the religious point of view, but they were concerned in that because they were concerned also in the maintenance of the true cearael:er of Eegland in all things that affected hcr probity, her manhood, and her purity. (Applause.) Mr. Wesley Cooke proposed a vote of thanks to the le3turer and chairman, and eepressed himself as being very much disappointed with toe attendance, and observed that it was remarkable that out of a dozen con- gregations they could only secure such a poor meeting. The Rev. C. W. L. Ceristiea seconded. He thought the attendance was rather a reflection on the Free Church portion of the townspeople. The Council had worked very hard to make that meeting a success, and they were not going to take tee blame for the small gathering.</p>
        <p>- The vote of thankci was carried unanimously, and the chairman re,ponded.</p>
        <p>A collection was t tken in aid of the Council's Funds, after which the proceedings terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>FREE CHURCH COUNCIL MEETING.</head>
        <p>FREE CHURCH COUNCIL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The Rev. R. Veitch on the Crisis in the</p>
        <p>Church.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening at Bethesda Chapel, the Rev R Veitch, M.A.. Liverpool, d4iverel a lecture upon "The Present Crisis: Our Free Chnrch Solution." Mr John Wilson presided, over a good attendance. There were also present : Revs C W L Christina (president of the Council) ; J Osborne secretary), and Mr Wesley Cooke (assistant secretary.) The Rev. J. Osborne said that lecture was one of three enterprzes which the Free Church Council had in hand at the present time. Tne second would be a lantern slide lecture on the life and work of Oliver Cromwell, to be given in the Public Hall at an early date, and the third enterprize would be a public gather- ing and demonstration early in the new year. By this programme they would understand that the Council was alive and well. If they would add to that the corres- pondence which had been published in the local papers with reference to the nurse fund they would see that the Council was not only alive and well, but, to use a slang phrase was also "kicking." But he wished to remind them of one thing, viz., that they were without any money at the present time. They were really without money, and he trusted they would all help heartily that ght. The Chairman said he was very glad to be present that evening, because they were al interested in the Evangelical Free Church movement. No doubt, had the night been warmer, they would have had a much larger audience, and he was only sorry there were not m ire present to welcome the lecturer. The history of the commercial and industrial life of the past few year had, in his judgment, taught the Free Churches the value of combination and co-operation, and had also shown what powerful c epacities and capabilities there were in such union for great achievements. The Free Church Council had taken the hint, and they were all now united. Whenever the history of the religious life of the last decade come to be written he was sure one of the brighte4t pages would be that on which they found the record of the birth and growth of the Evangelical Free Church movements. (Hear, hear.) It had not required any act of uniformity to bring that about—it had come into existence without anything of that kind—and it was spreading itself quietly and lovingly over all the country. They believed in love, and they believed in the inspired revelation of the Almighty Redeemer, the Divine Spirit, t the forgiveness of sin here and now, and the conscious knowledge of it; and in an if,heritance, incorruptible, undefiled, and that fadeth not away. They did not ad- mit the right of any priest or any other person to inter- fere between man and God save Jesus Christ. Truly, through Him they had redemption and through Him alone they received the forgiveness of sins and the power to love redemption. Their freedom con- sisted in this to some extent; they did not surrender the spiritual to the noliticel. Their churches were not allied to the State. Their unity seould enable them to be of greater value in brightening and sweetening the lives of the people; purifying the voice and action of municipal life, parliamentary life, national life and individual life, and of dealing more effectively with the drink question —(hear, hear)—and of tackling the immoral spirit of gambling, and dealing with vices and weaknesses in high places and low places. Let them be loyal to their principles, loyal to their Lord Jesus Christ. and hand down to poe'erity that freedom they had received, and if possible make it freer and better for those that came after them to enjoy it. (Applause.) The Rev. R.Vei-ch observed that the proper title of his lecture was "The Church Crisis: Our Free Church elution." He did not think that the church crisis was to be understood, that at any rate its seriousness was to be appreciated unless they understool how they cem. to it. He wanted to remind them that the Reformation found the least thorough impression in England of any of the refimmatinn cluntriee of Europe. They could not compare the theenuehnees of the Reformation that was carried out in England with the thoroughness of it in Scotland. Their reformation could not compare again in thoroughness with the reformation that was carri-d nut in Switzerland, in Holland, or in Germany; theutth in reference to the doctrine which taught of the Lord's Supper the Lutheran doctrine in some respects was closely allied to the doctrine of transubstantiation which a good deal underlay the doctrine of priestiem. Still the Lutheran Church in Germany had from the first had a popular form of Government. and had recognized the duty and privilege of the Christian laity. He contended that the Reform- ation in England was by no means as thorough as it was in the other reformation countries of Europe. If they would go back to the matter and take a casual view of it themselves, they would easily recognize that the refermetien was with them a kind of compromise. Henry VIII., whilst he was concerned to be king in his own land, and te assert his authority, as trad of the English Church, against the Pone, was also a good deal concern^d about another matter in which he personally wee interested, that was the justification of the rather long succession of his numerous wives' He did rpt mean to imply that he had no religious interest, but at any rate his religious interest had been a little under suspicion. There was not in operation a very deep religious zeal as the moving instrument in Englan 4 at the time of Henry VIII. Henry was too much concerned with political and private matters of his own to he out and out zea‘ous about reformation in religion. Following him was Edward VI and for a time there was a promise of thorough reformation in this land, but Eiwald lived too ehert a time and reigned but too few years for any stability to be given to the movement which was very zealously and very sincerely instituted on his behalf. Then they would remember that they went backward in the reign of Mary and that the coun- try underwent an experience which was summed up in the popular name which was given to her when she was described as "Bloody Marc'." She went to Romsnism and along with Rnmanism to the fires of Smithfield. Then they swung to Viz 4beth and the pendulum once more went to the other side, and they tried their hand once more at reformation. But they would remember that Elizabeth, great and noble Queen as she was from the English point of view, was only so far as reforma- tion was cencerned of a moderate disposition. After Elizabeth they had James I. and because of his Presbyterian antecedents in Scotland there seemed to be hope of reformation. but hn disappointed them. Then Charles I. came on the scene. He was on all occasions most difficult to get at, a man who was trying to play off nne power against another, and to ride upon their divisions to a larger newer himself. There was undoubt- edly in Charles' mind a leaning towards monastic revi-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE AND PARISH COUNCIL MEETINGS. —At the meeting of the fialtou Parocnial Committee, held at the Girls' School on Tuesday evening there were present—M,S3ra Thomas Wri,,,,ht,Joseph Stokes, J Acker - ley, J Mannincz, and G F Ashton (clerk to the Rural District CounA).—Tne Clerk reported the proceedingS of the Rural District Council with regard to the analysis of the Halton water, temarking that inasmuch as the Local Government Board had declined to undertake the analysis, tho Council had decided to submit samples to Prof. Sincthem,analyst to the Royal Agricultural;Society, Lancashire. The samples had been submitted but no report had ben received lip to the present.—With refer- ence to the sewerage scheme for Halton,a reply had been received from Mr. James Wilding (surveyor to the Run. corn Urban District Council)stating that the matter was underithe consideration of the Highway Committee of that body and until they had dealt with it he would be unable to fot ward his draft scheme.—lntimation was also received from the Cheshire County Council requir- kg that Oaklestons Tannery the Highfield Tannery should direct their sewage from the Manchester Ship Canal, and it was suggested in connectien with the scheme of couplinz on to the sewerage of Runcorn Urban ast:ict Council that some arrangement be made for the taking of the whole of the sewage of Halton, Brook.—Mr J Wright added that levels had been taken so that if D.eces=arY they could sewer th 3 whole of Halton. Mr J Manning presided at the Parish Council meeting held subsequently, but tbere was Tao businees of any importance.—Mr Stokes r. ported that the matter of the As:m)or Bridge Improvement was engaging the attention of the County Council.</p>
      </div>
