      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>MINOR OASES. -- Warrington Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES. -- Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>FAlDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair). Alderman Wright Councillor Srnethur.t. Dr. Gornall and Mr. llepherd. ALLEGED ATTEMPTED SUMIDE. Annie Kelly. of no fixed address who was remanded be Friday en a charge of at- tempting to commit suicide by jumping into the river at Arpley, w?s again bruught before the magistrates.—The Chief Constable again stated the faci,s of the case. hie said that when prisimer was before the Co .rt previously she did not seem to be at all peniten, and was remanded in order that she might get the 'rink cut of her. She now appeared to be In a better state of body and probably she would fake a differ- ent view of life.—Prisoner said she:had been left destitute hut aI4 not intend to comnait suicide. —The Chief Constable said th watnan did ant appear to have been respon4ibie for her actions and he would withdraw the char.ce. —Prisoner said she had friends at Manchester and the magistrates Instructed Mr Taylor, the Police Court Missionary, to get her a tailwaV ticket, and see her out of town. They also ordered a-b awl that prisoner had pawned to be redeemed for her. MONDAY—Before Mr. Birchall (in the chair), Councillors Shaw Green and Smethurst, and Messrs. S. Wright and</p>
        <p>W. Hayward. BEGGING.—Thamas Worsley or Johnson, was charged with begging in Sankey-street at eight o'clock en Sunday night. P.C. Stewart said prisoner who was under the influence of drink stopped several people and asked them for a penny for bis lodging- —Prisoner was sent to gaol for 7 days. BRWITIES.—The followinz were fined for having been drunk and disorderly :—James Duggan, Bank-street. ss; Joseph Miller, 2, Dial-court. 51 and costa; and Daniel Rosbothan. 10/I.—Thomas Rilcannon. a labourer, of Whitlows Farm. Thelwall, was fined as and costs for driving at night and not haying a light attache to his vehicle —Edward Hab- berley, Dial-court, was fined Is for allowing the chimney of his dwelling-howe to be on fire ors the 2nd inst. THEFT OFa. CLOCK. - Joseph Sitison, 21. Back Crossley- street, was charged with stealing from the dwellng-honse of James Johnson, 3 Rodney-street, a timpiece. value 201., at 3 o'clock on Tuesday the 21st of November.—The Chief Con- stable said that Johnson was a slater, and was a small employer of labour. The prisoner had been employed by him from time to time as a labourer. Ne was not in his employ at the time the theft took place. He had been in the habit of going to Johnson for " subs" and on the date in question he went to the house and asked. Mrs Johnson tor a " sub " She said she had nothing to give him. She then left the house. locking the front door. There was a consider- able sum of money in the house at the time. She WAS absent for a few minutes, and ois returning Nava astonished to ...ee prisoner going • out of the back door with some- thing under his coat. She shouted to him and be made some unintelligible reply and disappeared. She looked round the house and found that ths timepiece was miesing from the tnantlepieee. Information was given to the polfce and on the same day at 7 o'clock, Mrs Johnson went to prisoner's house and there saw the timepiece. She told prisover's wife that it belonged to her and she replied that her husband brought is home. He said he bad given a shilling for it Prisoner was apprehended on Sunday by Superintendent Monahan and when charged he said" don't know what made me take it, I was drunk."— Prisoner, who bad been before the Court many times previously, was sent to gaol for a month with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Messrs. _James Handley, (presiding), and</p>
        <p>F. J. Norman.</p>
        <p>UNIMPORTANT BUSINRSS.—The business before the court was of an unimportant nature —William Henry Ackerlv, was granted temporary authority to sell at the Royal Oak. Weston ; Thomas Norman, Robert-street, Runcorn, was fined 2s 6d for drunkenness Frederick Allman. Lowlands-road was fined 5s for allowing his dog at large ; and William Twigg for using obscene language in his house t Robert- street, was fined Be.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Newton Petty Session.</head>
        <p>Newton Petty Session.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.—Before Mr. T. J. Gillespie (in the chair),Colonel Wood. Mr C. B. F Borrou, and 11r. John Jaeluon, SHOCKING TR,ITMENT OF A. WIFE. -Wilfrel Mather, Mersey-street. Newton, was charged with persistent cruelty to his wife Esther Mather on dates previous to and on the let December, whereby he caused her to live apart from him.— Complainant said that her husband had been previously be- fore the Court, and promi,esd to sign the pledge. On the very same night, however, he got " beastly" drunk and she had to leave the hou-e and stay at a neighbour's because she was afraid of him. He had never used actual violence to- wards her, as she kad always run out of the way. Sometimes she had been out till 3 o'clock in the Morning with her two children. He had threatened to strangle her. On December Ist he came home and told her that tnere was no money for her. On her speaking to him he thr-atened to knc 2k her eyes out and she had to leave the house. He had told her that he could earn over £3 a week. she wanted a separation order, as she could not live with him any loneer. —Jane Horrabin, a neighbour, described defendant as being nothing but a seound,el. A Mn. Crompton said she had taken de- fendant's wife and children in her house, when he had turned them out.--After further evidence had been given, the magistrates granted a separation order, defendant to pay 10s a week to the maintenance of his wife and children. OFFENCES UNDER 'ME HXPLOSIVE9 Acr.—Edward Leach, was summon?d for having on the 31st October at Haydock, kept for sale upon his premises 167, Black Brook-road, 72tIbs of fireworks without having the shop registered contrary to the Explosives Act.—lnspeetor Sr ltt proved the case, and defendant was fined in the costs —John Beswick. 19, High- street, Golbarne, was fined in the costs for a similar offence. —lnspector Scott said that immediately over a paraffin oil tank in the shop ilvire were 211hs of gun powder and below the tank there was an open box contalnine 13lbs of fire works. Rakvrrtke.—Patrl,k Sexton was fined 5s and costs for allowing his horse and cart to stand in arket-stret,Barles— town, from 3.50 to 4.15 pm. on December sth, thereby causing an obstruction.—Jamea Kay was fined 2s 6,1 and costs for allowing a ho•rse and cart to stand in the street at Golborne from 5 to 53) p.m. on the 27th November.— William Collier was charged on a warrant with committing a breach of the peace by fighting at Haydosic on the sth November. —Thomas Houghton was charged with a like offence at Gelborne on t e 11th November.—Both defendants were bound over to keep the peace for six months in the sum of O.—William Farthimore was summoned fo assaulting William Pickton on the 9th December. After hearing the evidence, the magistrates di,missed the case.—The following were fined for drunkenness .—Patrioic Hovey (ninth appear- ance) lOs and costs ; Patrick Grady, Allen Wright, Thomas Priestley, Charles Fitotn, 2s 6d and costs.</p>
        <p>ALL COUNTRY PEOPLE should real The Rural Mrld id. weekly, which contains art Hes on Horses, Cattle. thie Dairy, Poultry, Bees, Rabbits, Pigs. Gardening', N4tura History, Botany of the Farm and Garden, the Home, Foreign and Colonial Intelligence. Anwers to Correspondents, etc. It is profusely illustrated and is altogether. a mast inte7 est- ing and instructive farming and country paper. A good medium for servants requiring situations. The Manager , offers to send a specimen tree. The address is 110 and I 111 STRAND, London, W.—Advt. " CLARKE'S WORLD-FAMED BLOOD ViDeriftiE " is a guaran- teed cure for all Bleed and Skin Dlselses. It is the most searching blood cleanser ever discovered. and it will free th system from all impurities from whatever cause arising. ForSerofula, Scurvy, Eczema, Bad Legs. Pimples and Sore of all kinds, its effects are marvellous. Thousanots of Testi menials. Sold everywhere, at 2s. 9d. per bottle. Beware 0 I worthless imitations and substitutes.— Advt. (I) HAVE YOU BOUGHT YOUR XMAS PRESENTS "No." Well realise the each by sending your ni.ll FALSE TEETH AND OLD BROKEN JEWELLERY. such as , Watch, s, Chains, Rings. Brooches. Bangles. also Antiques Curios War Medals, China and Pine Arts etc.. to I iverpco Gold and Silver Depot, who give you the best Market ISrices, or same.—Advt. Cures of Asthma, Coughs, &amp;c., BY DR. Loco* K'S PULMONIC WAFERS.—From MI% Oldham. Chemist. Market Place. Wts- beach.—From the great quantity of your Wafers I have sold. I have had an excellent opportunity of witnessing their effects, and I have much pieasu‘e in being able to inform you that several obstinate cases of asthma and coughs have been completely cured by their use; and.indeed,thcir efficacv Is general in diseases of the lungs." BE. LOCO,K.S WAFERS give Instant relief and taste pleasantly. Sold by ad Druggists at is. 10., 2s. 9d., 45.6 d. and its. per box.—A stet (5).</p>
