      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>I N view of the approaching Football Season. it may be in- I ter-sting to state that the world-famed manufacturers. GEO. G. BUBSEY &amp; On, of London. are preparing a stock o Balls, which for quality. uniformity, ant durability, will be{ unique. These balls will be distinguished by ths app-arance thereon of their well known trade mark Ask the local sports Dealer for their Catalogue. If not procurable, apply to 36 &amp; 38, Queen Victoria-street London. Factories —Peckham, London; awl Blms well, Suffolk.—Advt.</p>
        <p>*** Secretaries of Football Clubs are requested to note that reports of matches played on Saturday must be sent to the Head Office. Bewsey-street. Warrington, int later than 10 o'clock on Thursday morning or they cannot be inserted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>Warrington v. Morecambe.</p>
        <p>The friendly fix' ure decided on the Moss-lane enclosure between Morecambe and Warrington came off on Satur- day. Warrington took a strong team, compqsed as follows; Boscow, full back ' • Jones, Smith, Isherwood, and Fi-h, three-quarters ; Bate and Duckworth, half backs ; Taylor, Boardman, Fairhurst, Scholtze. Eden, Merryweather, Swift and Morrison, forwards. The out- set of the game was anything but interesting. Cer- tainly the " seasiders " came away nicely on several occasions, but they either failed at the critical period or were checked by Bosco w, who played a marvelously sure game at full bacg. Warrington, however, ulti- mately gained the upper hand,for Isherwood raced away in fins style, dodging some five or six of his opponents. Cornthwaite collared the flyer before he became danger- ous. Morecambe kept pegging away, but to get beyond Boscow was their biggest obitacle. Towards half-time a smart piece of work was accomplished by Duckworth and Fish, ending in the latter scoring the Mot try, which was improved by Jones. When the interval came Warrington led by five points ta nothing. The second half was of an exciting charac- ter, for within a few minutes from the commencement Campbell rushed down and scored a try clean beneath the posts. Elkin made a poor attempt to put the leather over. , This put new life into the "seasiders,", and they pressed the Warrington defence very hard. This,•how- ever, was not for long. All round the visitors showed better football, and after several smart movements .4mith romped over. Levi, Jackson' Woodhouse, and Wilson were conspicuous with several individual good movements, but they all proved fruitless. Towards time the game degenerated somewhat, for rough play was in- dulged necessitating the interference of the referee time after time. "Smack" Bate ag tin scored. and Jones im- proved, and Warrngton retired winners by 13 points to three.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION. More Points For Runcorn.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION. More Points For Runcorn.</p>
        <p>Runcorn sent their selected team to Rochdale, and captured the expecte 1 number of points. When the visitors arrived at Rochdale they found that the Dane- street enclosure was too hard to play upon, but the old St.:Clernent's ground being protected by Nag grass was in better condition.so the match was fought out at Spot- land. The Hornets had re-arranged their forward divi- sion after the fiasco of the previous week, including Longhorn. of Dalton, and Trembath, of Cornwall (who was brought to Rechdale as a centre three-quarter. Harrison and Wood also reappeared the team. When Farmer kicked off the Hornets had only fourteen men on the ground, but Boothroyd appeared five minutes later. The Hornets, as is their custono,went away with a dash, but just as the position was getting interesting to the spectators Houghton got hold, and drove them back to the centre. Again the "Insects" swooped down, and Field, sca,pping thp ball up hastily, threw to Bcotlaroyd, but with a clear course he missed the ball. A plucky effort by Davies was fol- lowed by a passing movement by the visitors three- quarters and Jolley. Boothroyd taking advantage of a mull, dribbled down. When the critical moment arrived the hall rolled into touch—the Hornets' usual luck. As Chadwick carried the ball over this line the hopes of the Rochdalians ran high, but again they were dashed to the ground, for the ball was kicked from under him as he was dropping on it. A long kick past Houghton gave the Hornets another opening, but Har- rison was rather too slow. Right up to half-time Roch- dale pressed, but nothing fell to them beyond dead bal:a and the chaage of ends took place with nothing scored Resuming, the Hornets had several chances, but mulled them through over-eagerness. For ten whole minutes the Runcorn players did nothing but tackling. Then, to relieve the monotony some scrummaging took place on the home line, and Faulkner wriggled over. Houghton made a capital kick, but the position was too difficult. Having got the lead. Runeorn played up better, being helped tremendously by 11,ughton'Ei powerful kicks' and strong running. From a punt by Rigg he" market," and dropped a goal from between the " 25" line, and inidfigld. To complete the Harnets' discomfiture, Hardiker was hurt and had to leave the field. He came on again later' but was of little service. Warder added another try, Houghton again being at fault with the kick. Encouraged tly a clever piece of play on the part of Field and Benthroyd. the Hornets made one last effort. It proved unavailing, however, and the whistle blew with the score :—Rukc irn 1 goal 2 tries (13 points); Hornets, nothing. Salford v. Millom.</p>
        <p>This match, which was decided at Salford, resulted in a win for the home fifteen by on,, goal and flue tries to two g3sl3 and twl tries. Parson (three), Hadwen and T Williams s.cured th winners' tries. PhiUip goaling one •, while Lathom and G Whitehead scored for Millom. Buckett kicking both goals. Swinton v. Wigan,</p>
        <p>A large crowd wined tnis game h t Swinton, play being of a finctuating character. Eventua.lly Swinton gained a try by Lewis, which gave them the victory b 7 three points to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>St. Helens V. Oldham.</head>
        <p>St. Helens V. Oldham.</p>
        <p>At St Helens,before nearly 10,000 spectators. The band of the 2nd Lancashire Engineeri previous to the match rendered selections of music, and the boys of the Church Lads' Brigade were busy collecting for the Mayor's Transvaal Fund. St. Helens turned out as selected. Appleton an old forward played half back in place of Briers. The visitors had their full strength. Foulkes won the toss, and Frater kicked off for Oldham. It was at 03C0 evidenced that the St. Helens forwards were in grand form. Liversedge scored a try within three minutes from the start amid great enthusiasm. Traynor failed at goal. Appleton picked up from a loose dribble, and scored a grand try. Foulkes struck the upright with his kick at goal. Boyle secured and passed to Appleton, who feinted to pass to Dohetty. and are tricked his opponents and scored another try. Foulkes madl a po n. kick at goal. A minute or two later. white the home forwards were going up the field, Traynor dasht dup and secured. He easily rolndeal Thomas and added another try. Imme- diately after wards Traynor secured the ball near t'e centre and dropped a splendid goal. Half-time : St. Helens, 1 goal, four tries; Oldhsna, nil. Traynor kicked over the lino, Boyle dashing up and grounding in magni- finent style, but the goal kick failed. After a stoppage through an injury to Thherty, the Oldham men by a loose dribble went to the home line, where after several scrambling scrimmages in front of the home goal S. Lees scared an easy try. The same player kicked a goal. St. Helens now fell away very much, and the Oldham Lacks were allowed to get in some pretty runs. S. Lees went over once more, but no go al restarted. Fletcher raced over, but the goal kick failed. Davies ran in a splendid try, the goal-kick being successful. Final : St. Helens, 17 points; Oldham, 14. points. Leigh v. Widnes.</p>
        <p>On the ',sigh ground. Both teams were short. Bown, Rispan.and Hughes were absentees from the visitors. About 2,000 witnessed the start. Davies was butt in a nharge. The visite, s pressed, and Kinsella dropped a fine goal. Aspey made his mark from the restart, and Molyneux btaited a passing bout, but offside sp-Aled the effort. Molyneux drappA a goal from a penalty kick. Ganley scored for Leigh, M•Ayneux's kick failing. Half-time :—Leigh 5 points, Widnes 2 points. After the restart Ganley cc 'red again from a fine pass by Garvey. Molyneux again missing. Prlenek and Neaitt Ecord for the visitors. Result :-8 points each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>YORKSHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>YORKSHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Castleford v. Leeds Parish Church.</p>
        <p>These teams mPt 'at Leeds, and for the home latin• ation LeWi3 h:ellid a penalty-goll and sccercd two triPs whilst fiewlitt added another try. Nel dropped a goat for CastMori, who losr, by a goal to cue gz..at and tone tries. Leeds v. Hull Kingston Rovers.</p>
        <p>A well-contested game was witnesed at LeE di, 1 h visitors gaining a narrow victory by two tries to tIA goals (six points to four). Morfitt and Kemp ran over the winners' tries, and Regan and Willey dropped gals for the losers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>Liverzedge v. Manningham.</head>
        <p>Liverzedge v. Manningham.</p>
        <p>All the voring in tnis avitch at Livereedge took placs in the initial half, victory nit 14!Y resting with Man- ningham by one goal and three tree :i :Ail.</p>
        <p>Brighouse Rangcrs v. Hunslet_</p>
        <p>This match cm; p,ayed on the !zround ot the Ringer 3, who secured the victory, atier a lt; 211 st7ugglc, by one goal (two points) to nil.</p>
        <p>Hull v. Bradford.</p>
        <p>The result of a keen struggle at Hull was a vcell- deserved win for the visitors by a goal and two tries to one try. .</p>
        <p>Ho!beck v. Bramley.</p>
        <p>At the cinse ot this game at Holbeck the local fifteen were returned the winners by one goal and three tries to one try.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. CASTLE HILL.--tit Warrington. The play opened even, and nothing of moment occurred until a fine run by Hampson caueed Asplnall trouble. Fol- lowing a fine clearance came a raid on the visitors' goal, and a dropping shot by Connor almost took effect. Hotchkiss was kept busily ethployed, and capital work by Stunders and 11I'Grev caused further trouble to the Castle Hill defenders. Warrington continued to do the major portion of the attacking until Isherwood and Harnpsen obliged with clever play, and the game raged Inside the Warringtou quarters: Hot work was wit- nessed, but the defence was superior to the attack. and M'Grey was exceptionally prominent in beating off the vain, endeavour of the visitors to open the scoring account. Connor centred splendidly for the Warring- I toniana a moment later, and the Castle sill goal had a ' marvellous escape. The game continued exciting, and hands "against Hackett saw Whittle centre well, but the clearance was effected. Offside play against Castle Hill realised nothing. Avpinall was applauded for a really brilliant save, which brought him to his knees. Castle Hill were now going great guns, and Aspinalt. Mather, and Glover were kept busily employed. Relief came through M'Grey and Pierpoint.• an :I the venue being quickly' changed a bet frisilsd, was kept on the Castle goal. Fallowing some fine play by Murray the locals again showed determinqion, and the outcome of fine play by Pierpoint saw that player score a fine goal, this being the only score at the interval. The searind half was opened by Warrington in promising style, for _clever forwAr3 work resulted •in Pierpolat flatting the net, but alleged offside play rendered Ilia goal negative. Soon after Castle Hill equaliz,d through the aid of Marsh, and despite the energies of the locals they failed to regain the lead, and the eqd arrived with the score a goal each. Extra time was played without any score being netched, and the match will accordingly be replayed on the ground of the Castle Hill club on a date to be aecidel upon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, DECEMBER 15, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, DECEMBER 15, 1899.</p>
        <p>Wakefield Trinity v. Halifax.</p>
        <p>Metcalf opened the scoring in thig match at Wakefield by securin:i a goal from a mark, afcer which Brock well ran over a try, the homo team thus winning by a goal and a try to nil.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON " A " V. FLIXTON.—At Warrington, the visitors had all the best of the play, and at half-time Flizton had eight points to their credit. In the second half Warrington played a little better, and Moores scored a try, Resnit;:—Flixton, one goal two tries: Warrington, min try:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>LANCASHIRE JUNIOR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ST. MARY'S V. LOSTOCIC GRALAM Played at Warrington on Saturday. Both teams were well represented, the home 'side getting together their best men. Tne'vieitors won the toss, and Ashton com- menced, and through the visitors failing to return, a scrimmage was formed near the quarter flag. May was responsible for a grand clearance t 9 centre, where, f some time, even play ruled. A free kick was allowed the Saints for an obAruction, and a dash for the line almost ended in Lee, scoring for the home team. Billington relieved, the home team being forced back into, their own half, and shortly afterwards Smith saved a determined rush by the visitors. Lowe cleared, and Faulkner again came away, but Smith easily accounted for him. From a scrimmage which followed Lowe got away in fine style, and after running half the length of the fiell was grassed near the line. Another clearance was effected by Billington, but the home ,team still maintained the upper hand, although they could not score. May restarted, and cach end was visited, but only brief stays were made. After ten minutes' play St. Mary's pressed, and the outcome was that Potter scored, the kick at goal Wing a failure. Good runs were numerous, each side having it share, but the home team had the luck, and shortly before the conclusion of the game Lawless obtained a pass at centre, and dodging several players, succeeding in scor- ing a second try for St. Mary's. Final scorn :—Warring- ton St. Mary's, two tries (six points) ; Lostock Graham, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL. --- – WIGAN CUP.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL. --- - WIGAN CUP.</p>
        <p>Haydock v. Newton-le-Willows.</p>
        <p>For the Eecind year in succession these clubs have teen drawn against each other in the first round for Wic,an and District Cup, the tie being considered an easy task for Ha.ydock, who had choice of ground. Ha.ydock had a new player at inFide left in Fetcher, ,of Astley Bridge. Roberts stlrtel play for Haydeck. but Newton rushed away and Hill then scored for them. Haydock were not long in equh.lisin,7„ Mee doing the trick with a arand shot. Newton played up with dash and were all round the Haydock men.and they beat Les again,though it nughll to have been off for offside. Haydock forced a corner, and the ball was sent in the Let a second time. A free kick was thi downfall of the Newton goal a third time. Hall-time—Haydock 3, Newton 2. Final, Hay- dock 4, Newton 2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Earlestown v. Adlington.</head>
        <p>Earlestown v. Adlington.</p>
        <p>In this tip, at Adlington, Eirlestown, were first to show up, and after two fruitless corners they scorei. Adlington then took up the play and Heywood equalised with a splendid ht.F. aargreaves getting a second and Chadwick a third. Earlestown now bf,gan to improve, r,nd owing to bad play by Corner. the home 133 ck, they got the ball through Corner putting them another through his own goal. Blackburn having no chance. This compietely disheartened the borne team, and far a time E trlestown hvi rather the best of the play. In the second 13,1 f Eatlestown were without Parr, who got his leg hurt just before half-time, and the home team taking advantage of their we .kened state pressed and scored two more goil, leaving the final score—A llington, five ; Earlestown, three. 11.-r Adlingt,-n Heywood, Jolley, Ilargreaves, and Harrison were perhars the nick, while for Etrlestown Parr, up to being injurecl,wai the shining</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>LANCASHIRE LEAGUE. RESULT 3 UP TO SATURDAY, DECEMBER 9.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE LEAGUE. RESULT 3 UP TO SATURDAY, DECEMBER 9.</p>
        <p>Goals.</p>
        <p>Played. Won. Lost. Drawn, For. Agst. Crewe Alexandra l3 ... 8 ... 3 ... 2" .. 37 ... 21 ... 18 Stockport County lO ... 7 ... 2 ... 1 ... 23 ... 10 ... 15 Darwen 8 . 5 ~. 0 ... 3 ... 13 ... 6 ... 13 Stalybridße Rovers 9 ... 6 ... 2 ... 1 ... 13 ... 7 ... 13 Blackpool Haydock Southport Ceatral Cho-ley south Shore Wigan County lO . 2 ... 3 ... 5 ... 9 ... 11 ... 9 Rarlestown Middleton White Star Wan'd Roohdalo 10 . 3 ... 6 ... 1 ... 10 ... 26 ... 7 Horwich l4 ... 1 ... 10 ... 3 ... 11 ... 30 ... 5 South Liverpool Earlestown v. Middleton.</p>
        <p>The initial league fixture between these clubs was brought off on toe Earlestown ground on Saturday in grand football weather. Earlestown were unfortunate in in-; ig the toes and had to play down hill in the first half. Hampeon kickei off, and after one or two smart exchanges Bylance by a miskick let in Barnes who in a good pesition shot for goal, but sent over the bar. From the goal kick,Middletou were k 13t in their own g rarters, and Barnes had again ano her opining, but his shot went yards wide of the upright. These were bath easy chances, and with a little care should have been con- verted into goal. Middleton invaded the home territory, and Heyes and Cottarn were kept busy for a few minutes. Johnson sricked in, and clearrd,and Settle and Leach getting position worked down. Settle sent across and Barnes meeting, made no mistake this time, but shot stro tgly into the net. 14 ro-n the kick off, Middleton went away with a rush, and Chew's charge seemed] in danger, but Hues oppnrtunely cleared and play was immediately tr insferrai zo the other end,where a corner was forced, but nothing came of it. A free kick enabled Middlettin to ret up, but the attack was repelied. They again invaded the hone terr:tory, and same good pas- sing enabled them to get dangerously near Cnew, bat Reyes hiaded the wrong side of the upright. Again Middleton swarmed round the home citadel, and Chew jest mana-!ed to save a high shot from Leigh,and imtne- diatelr after Blakiley sent a stinger which Chew had difficulty in getting away. The venue was changed by Settle, who eluded all opposition, hut Rylance spoiled the off ort by kickin;;; out. The throw in was worked across to B :tines who grazed the crws- bar, and Middleton got beyond the half way line. They were sent back and S.ttle troirbled Pollard with a shot. Jest before half-time a bully in front of the Middleton coal resulted in Flampson touch- ing the ball past Pollard. and when the whistle blew, the gain,. stood E nilestown 2. Middleton 0, Oa crossing over Middleton attacked with energy, having the hill in their f-avour,and their efforts were rewarded by Heyes scorinz with a simple shot which Chew made no at- tempt t save. From this point they were kept contin- ually on the ditfensisre,and Leach SCO-A,l from a splendid centre by Barnes. Toe gams was all in favour of E tr- -1,44'.0 wn ald Settle scored the fourth goal, the rem! b )ing Earlesto wn 4 goals, Mid lleton 1. Earlestown Rovers v, Castner Kellner.</p>
        <p>Played at Runcorn on Saturday. Ellison started o-) behalf of the Ravers before a fair gate. Castner prresed at first but were soon driven hack, and some nice play by Ellison,reaulted in twat player striking the upright with a fast low shot—disrirctly hard lines for the Rovers Cotner a,zain • took up thA running. Da.viPs missing his kick, the home centre forward was loft with an rpen trAal. He shot hard and 8 night at Ashcroft, the custorliaa bringing off a redly riaericlid save. The ball was SOIR hovering riund the home goal again. and Jenkinson eventually s:oral qv, first and only goal of the match for the Ilwr.r9. fialf-jme Itays, 1 ; Castner Kellner, nit. During the s-cand pardon the lymnesters hal pr .ctically the whole of the play, but failed to pierle the Rwers defence, the result being : Rovers one : Castiv:r IK,4lner, nil.</p>
