        <p>NOTES ON GAMES and ATHLETICS. Rugby Football. . [By "Ovalist."] The fact tlat the match at Morecambe was only a " fri-ndly," and the biter coldness of the day, preven- ted me making the spaside trip this year. I thought our famous" A" team vvould provide quite enough enter- taitment at Ftixton's expease,so I spent the afternoon at Wilderspool. The entertainment did not come off, and when I add that our vaunted "second string succumbed ingloriously, you will understand why. Elaving seen the "A "team once, Ido not feel inclined to see them any more, if Saturday's display was tiny criterion of their form. A more disjointed slovenly fifteen surely never took the field. Perhaps the unimportance of the match bad something to do with Wartington's play—or lack of it—but they nevertheless devrve severe castiga- tion for losing so ignominiously to a team they have already beaten. AU credit must be given to Flixton„ however. For a junior organisatioa they played very well indeed, and ,their determination, dash, and smart- ness should win them more than a few mateltel.</p>
        <p>Though I was not at Morecambe, "+a little bird" told mc all I require to know about what went on there. To begin with the ground was cl,cidedly hard, and more than one mgmber of the team came back with one or two spots requiring Homocea or something else. Then to the game was hard, for the Morecambe lads put in all they knew in an effort to prove tow.; they were still fit for the premier division. The effort %cal vain for the strong team which Warrington took away was more than a m tch for the seasiders.</p>
        <p>Fish opened the scoring from the opposite wing to big own. Hew he got thera seemed a bit of a msstery to the Morecambrians, but there he was, and he very promptly scoreJ in his inimitable style. Jones kicked a goal, and then things got lively. Morecambe wanted to do likewise, and just after the int-uval, they rushes the bill over and gct within two paints of Warrington. &amp;me roughtL. was infwed into tha play—there generally is at Morecambv—but Smith was 'Jot long in increasing the lead with his first try for Warrington. He picked up from a cross kick and raced in smartly. Bite aided a thud, and Jone 3 kicked another goal, bringing the total to points, against Morecambe's three paints.</p>
        <p>Boscow, Fish, and Smith ware the best men on the Warrington nide, the full hack playing a wonderfolly goNi game all through. Fish was irrepressible. He ran and dodged and kicked in his best form, and the Morecambe men found the utmost difficulty in locating him, while tackling was nearly out of the question. Even the spectators gut irritated at Fish's cool manner of eluding his foes. Smith played a sound clever game, and when he gets neat to Fish—as I hope he will shortly —the left wing should be particularly dangerous. The new style of playing arlowed by Bate and Duckworth at Leigh was again successful, and resulted in sonae very effective passing. Jones seems to have found his goal kicking to)is again. Let us hope they are not lost any more.</p>
