        <p>St. Helens have a splendid record, and in improving it against O'clham they prows' their recent successes to be no mere "'lobes in the pan." The team is a well- balanced one as at pres.ent constituted, but the lack of reserve men of any class may tell a tale shortly. In any case the struggle next week when St. Helens come for the point Warrington took in the opening match this season, will he exceptionally keen. Runcorn still keep at the had of affairs and show no signs of coming down yet. Warrington'i position is fairly good, but if the next two matches 'are won—snd they ought to be— lt will be nearer th-! place where one wishes it to be,</p>
