      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>MR. CROSFIELD'S GIFT TO TOMMY ATKINS.</head>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD'S GIFT TO TOMMY ATKINS.</p>
        <p>There has just been shipped on board the White Star steamer Majestic, for the use of the British troops in S iuth Africa, one ton of tobacco, in about equal pro- portions of bright cavendish and black cavendish. It was formed into cakes, each weighing four ounces, for convenient distribution among the men and was packed in eighty packages, this latter subdivision into small parcels being necessary for easy transports to the front on mules' backs This ton of tobacco it may be remem- bered was the gift to the British Army of Councillor Crosfield. and the execution of his generous purpose was entrusted to Messrs Celle Bros. and Co., of Liverpool, by Mr. Richard Toft, of Warringt-m. No doubt" Tommy Atkine," aa he lac?s him,:elf with bright cavendish and the famous "Old Crow," will feel grateful to Mr Crosfield for his considerate generosity. This, however, is by no means the only benefaction of the same kind. The gift of Mr. Crosfield is only on of many in which generous cit'zens have endeavoured t ) mitigate the hardships of the campaign by making handsome presents of tobacco and coats for the use of:officera and men on the voyage to the Cape and during service in the field. Among the doisors may be mentioned the members of the London Stack Exchange, who sent oar a large quantity of eaveradish tobacco. The Earl of D)rby was eqeally con- , s'derate in placing a laze parcel of tobacco. cigars, and pipes on board each of the transports Isnaore and Columbian. The residents of Birkenhead, through Mr. Cecil Holden and Mr. lye Fulton,sent out about I,oooibs of cake tobacco and some clears in the transports Pindari, U mston orange and Rapidan, whilst in other directi ns Colonel Howarth, of the 3rd V.B. Lancashire Fusiliers; Mrs R ChamberlaM for tee London Ladies' Fund ; Mr J Sin field, of Scarborough ; Mr Wheeldon of Loughborough; Mrs Abdy of Old Charlton; Mr P B Harris, of London, and various others have forwarded consA..rable quantities of tobacco for the use of our soldiers in South Africa. The whole of these orders have been entrusted to Messrs Cope Bros. and Co.</p>
      </div>
