      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>SHE: "I thought you told me your salary was £lO a week?" He: "Oh, no; I said I earned ten, but I only get three."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <p>"QUAINTLY talks like a man who has travelled all over every country on earth." "Never was out of Middlesex in his life, but he has the finest collection of guide-books in existence." THE prisoner's dock is not a situation favourable to repartee. But on once • occasion the accused undoubtedly scored. " Why did you manufacture this bad money?" the magistrate said, sternly. "'Cos I couldn't turn out no better," replied the counterfeiter. Mr. OLDDI :"I am a sell-made man, sir. I began life as a barefoot boy." Kennard: "Well, I wasn't born with shoes on, either." • QUESTIONER-" Is that clock right over there ?" Grumper—" It doesn't look as though it were any- where else." ZIGGSBY "I tell you, my boy, I don't handle the money I used to.' Perksby : "How is thao " Ziggsby : "My wife handles it." " GEORGE describes the girl he is engaged to as 'a perfect vision." "Yes. And his sister says she is a sight." , ENCOURAGING.—Fred: "He married the girl I was engaged to." Arthur: "Well, don't worry. You'll get over it before he does." • WILLIE SLIMSON : "Mamma said she hoped you'd call to-day." klrs. Von Biumer "Where is your mother?" Willy: "She's gone shopping." IT is said that a man could easily carry a million pounds if the money was in £l,OOO notes. It is worth while for every man to know this, so as to be prepared for an emergency. A CHILD OF Tilil TIMES.—" Why, . Frankie, what are you reading in that book about bringing up children?" "I'm just looking to •see whether I'm being properly brought up " MISS BORELY: "I called on Mrs. De Vere to-day.". Miss Cutting : "So did I." Miss Borely : "She was looking very tired, didn't you think so ?" Miss Cutting: " Yes ; she said you had just called.'i GUEST: "I wish I had come hero a week ago." Hotel Prope'setor : " Ah, that's very flattering to my establisinnent." Guest : "I don't know about that. What I mean is, that I should have pre- ferred to eat this fish then instead of now." A little girl five year old was told by her teacher that the :I,l.dssippt River was called the father of waters. "How is that Miss Mattie ?" she queried "If it is the father of waters, it ought to be Mister.</p>
        <p>DISAPPOINTED HER. Edith: "I understand that you and George are not friends now?" Mabel: "No ; we have quarrelled." Edith : "What about?" Mabel: "Oh, I don't know what, but Pm sure I wouldn't have done it if I'd thought he wouldn't have offered to make it up." DID HIM GOOD—" Mister," said the small Lo.; to the druggist, "gimme another bottle o' them patent pille you sold father day before yesterday:' "Are they doing him good?'. asked the assistant, looking pleased. • "1 d'no whether they're doin' father good or not, but they're doing me good They just fit my new pop-gun." IN THE CHEMICAL LABORATORY.—" Professor, what has become of Tom Appleton? Was'nt he studying with the class last year?" " Ah, yes; Appleton—poor follow I A fine student, but abseat-minded in the use of chemicals—very. That discoloration on the ceiling—notice it?" " Yes." " That's him." CORRECTLY DESCRIBED.L—A story is told of a nervous man whose duty it was to move a vote of thanks after a lecture. He floundered along through various complimentary sentences, and finally flickered out feebly thus; "And so I pro- pose a vote of thanks for the lecture to which we have so ably listened ! " AN INFALLIBLE RULE.—Brobson : "Uncle Stayles is a man from whom I have expectations, you know; but I see I'm alienating him by not laughing at the right place in his stories. How am Ito manage?" Crair : "Why, it's dead easy; get close enough to him so he can nudge you." INVIGORATING. Mrs. Smith (thoughtfully) : "I'm afraid I shall have to stop giving Bobby that tonic the doctor left for him." Mr. Smith</p>
        <p>(anxiously) : "Why, isn't he any better?" Mrs. Smith "Oh, yes. But he has slid down the banisters six. times this morning, broken the hall lamp, two vases, a pitcher, and a locking-glass, and I don't feel as if I could stand much more."</p>
        <p>UTILIZING THE WoLF.—His faithful wife strove to comfort the starving humourist. "Take heart," she urged, "and write." He gazed at the bare, cheerless walls and fireless hearth. "I cannot," he murmured. "Even now the wolf is at the door !" With the words a sudden inspiration darted into his face. Grasping an axe, he crawled through a cellar window and fell upon the animal from behind. That night the humourist, after the first steak dinner he had eaten for weeks, wrote eighty-eight wolf jokes.</p>
        <p>ESTABLISUING AN ACQUAINTANCE. —A little Irishman smoking a short clay pipe entered a crowded public-house, walked up to the bar, elbowed several customers aside, took a match from the match-stand on the counter, lit his pipe, and then walked out without a word. The astonished barman gazed after him in amazement and audibly wondered who he was. The very next morning the Irishman walked into the same place, made his way to the counter, lit his black pipe, and started out again. As the little man reached the door, the barman recovered his voice suffi- ciently to call out: "I say! who are you?" The Irishman turned round and said: "You know me." Then he walked out. The barman said that he would lie in wait for the intruder the next day. At about the same hour he reappeared, helped him- self to a match as usual, and lit his short pipe. He was about to turn away, when someone grabbod him by the lappel of his coat. " Who are you, now?" de—ianded the barman. "Oh, you know me," repli, • little Irishman. "No, I don't," said the man white apron. "Who are you?" " Wuy,:- • the cool reply, "I'm the man that conies in hc,.. light his poipe ivory marnin'."</p>
        <p>A WORRIED WO7,TAN.—" Oh, dear," sighed Mrs. Junius, shaking her head. "I wish I could make up my mind what to do with that old green dress of mine. Here I've been thinking over it day and nigh c for a week, and I declare Pin more muddled than when I began." Mr. Junius stirred medita- tively with his spoon. " Your mind," he said, "is like this coffee." " I'd like to know, Julius, what you mean by that," cried his wife, staving the piece of meat half-way to her lips, " if indeed there's any meaning to it at all which generally I'm sure there isn't and especially comparing your wife to things to drink is something no true gentleman would be guilty of nor would I have ever married you if I had supposed I was going to be insulted at my own table for well you know it is and bonght, with money I saved by not doing the washing at a laumlry where they charge doubre.not to tnention blueing in the shirt, bosom making them streaked and you laying the blame on to me like every man who has a patient-, wife. Oh yes, Julius, that's all very well for you to sit there grinning like a graven image at that napkin-ring but far more decent would it be for you to own up to seine of toe help you get from me slaving -my finger tips away at home with you spending your money on your cheap companions in town and thinking up things to say about your wife's mind and then all of you laughing at the poor faithful women wearing out their lives and aprons wishing dishes and turMng dresses like this green one of mine so that our husbands can have new overcoats while we go along the streets all winter having people who are rich pointing the finger of scorn at us and you caring no more than the wind that blows about exciting my curiosity over your foolish conundrums else if you did why would you sit there so dumb Julius winking at that plate of toast with inn caring not the least thing about it except your aggravating way of not explaining why it is that my mind is like that coffee." Mrs. Junkie, here remembering the piece of tne.t on her fork, stopped her mouth with it,. and her husband seized the advantage. "Your mind is like this coffee," he said, directing another contemplative glance into his cup, "because it is so dreadfully unsettled." And above the dull rumbling of a passing furniture van Mr. Junius could clearly discern the comments of his—er— better half. THE GLEN-SPEY AND STRATHMILL DISTILLERIES Situated in the finest Whisky-producing district of Scotland re the property of W. and A. Gilbey, and the QV hisky made from Horn, -grown Barley only. Glen-Spey," 3s. 6d nd —6trathmill." 3s. 6d.. scAl by W. and A. GILBEY. gents in every town.—Advt.</p>
