        <p>It is the delicate children which cause the most anxiety, and especially thAe the are al ways ailing without what seems to be any reasonable cause. In the condition of health the foregoing seutencs sug- gests, it is usually absAutely unnecenary to ad- minister powerful drugs or tonics ; their rffects are not lasting,and tbey often do more harm than good. The best way to treat these cases is revealed in the following letter "7, Blunrlell-road. Sparkhill, • "Birmingham,</p>
        <p>" March Ist, 1899.</p>
        <p>" Gentlemen,—l feel it my duty to write and tell you of the very great benefit my daughter—aged five years and six months—has derived from your remedy Scott's Emulsion, which in her case has thoroughly justified all you say about it. Lt Match she was laid up with a bad attack of iinfluenza and bronchitis, and although she was pulled through by a competent medical man, ske nerei seemed to re- cover her previous good health and spirits. On waking in the morning her tongue would he coated over and lips rough and sore, and the child never seemed to enjoy her tood and always appeared ail- ing. In January of this year I determined to try the effects of Scott's Emuls!on, and afVr the first few doses she took it readily and without after-effect— as was the case with pure oil when tried—and I soon began to notice a change for the better both in her general appearance and appetite. She woke up in thP mommy in a more cturrful</p>
