        <p>POl, DON'T CALL</p>
        <p>the li,7ttle olack. Wash sor own lat,n leave the Kettle alone. Mend your own manneis, then use your soare time mending other folks. Bald- headed men should not recommend Lair restorers, and faxes "caught in the act" in the hen roost should not keture on honesty.. Preachin: cream and praczising skim milk may deceive Simple &amp;MOWS first cousin, but it won't answer with ex- perienced greyneaidi. Pot, cetse van chattering or you'll go to Pot. " Going to pot " through ill- health is not a consummation devoutly to be wished, and evils such as Indigestion, Wind on the Stomach,Liver o.mplaints,Sick Headacise,Costive- ness, Nervous Debility, Palpitation of the Heart, (Un., soon send men arid' women in a wrong direc- tion, but Page Woodcock's Wind Pills bring about marvellous changes. If in good time they took Page Woodcock's Wind Pills this premature going to pot would be avoided. We're sui of it.</p>
        <p>Mr. James White, Fore-street, St. Ives, Cornwall, writing to his friend, Mr. Thomas Smith, 132, Fleet, London, says :—.l Speaking with a Chemist a day or two since he to,d me he used to sell about a dczsri boxes of Page Woodc ck's Wind Pills a month, now he gets rid of 3 doz,ma A week." Reads well, doesn't it. Lo3ks as though the Cornish folk appreciated the best we can give them —P. W.</p>
        <p>Every bard-driven business man, every tired weary, housewife, every working man and woman, often prevented by their working conditions from taking sufficient exercise, should take Page Wood- cock's Wind Pills.</p>
        <p>Page Woodcock's Wind Pals being purely V(ge- table, Tasteless,and Mild arid Tonic in their action, may be taken with perfect safety by the most delicate.</p>
        <p>Page Woodcock's Wind Pills are sold by all Medicine Vendors at le. lid. and 24. 9d. ; post free for price by Page Woodcock, Lincoln.</p>
