      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>Magisterial Intolerance.</head>
        <p>Magisterial Intolerance.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am eu—rprised that the champion of local Liberalism, to wit, his worship the Mayor, b 11,mi 1d be at the bottom such an arrant Conservative. I was always under the impression that Liberalism meant that a man was to be allowed to hold his own opinions, but it seeriae we are now living in times when there is no difference bstween Liberalis and 'Tory ism—they both mean that men are not to think for th, mselves, but to let other people think for them, and dictate what they most do. We had an illustration of this at the last November election, and as if to show how strongly this pernicious doctrine possesses his worship, the Mayor must needs seek to enforce it in the temple of justice. I have often thought the Warrington magistrates have overstepped their duty in venturing to Lecture the applicants for certificates of exemption f:om vaccination, but I think when they conduct themselves as they did a fortnight ago in quitting the bench rather than do their duty, they merit the severest censure. Their duty is plain. The Act of Parliament says that appli- cants for exemption "shall satisfy" the magistrates that they have a "conscientious objection to vaccination" as prejudicial to the health of their children, and if the justices are not sati!fied, all they have to do is to say so, and the matter ends. But it is both unmaely and un- dignified to shirk the duty they have undertaken to dis- charge, viz :—the dispensing of justice simply because of preconceived ideas. Verily how true it is that it is difficult to do justice to these wtio differ from us, but, nevertheless, justice has to be done. His Worship the Mayor had great difficulty over the "conscientious" objtction.and this was one of the pciuts so ably urged by Mr. Labouehere when this matter was before the HOUS. of Commons. How are we to determine a"conscientious" objection? What will satisfy the Mayor? Apparently nothing. His mind is made up, and he wants nothing further. But that is scarcely the attitude of a dispenser of "justice," therefore we must endeavour to solve the problem. Well, I should think that a man who leaves his work,—and, it may be loses his wage in con- sequence, to go and ask for a certificate shows Some evidence of a conscientious objection : but that is not enough. he must "satisfy." Therefore, what will satisfy? The Mayor evidently thinks that a man should be able to express his cenecientious objection as fluently as he can speak himself. But all men are rot so gifted, and thus allowances must be made or them. If a man could not give a reason for the faith that is in him, but stated that he would rather go to jail than suffer his children to be vaccinated, that, I take it, would be con- scientious enough. There would be no getting beyond this. Still, we must remember that the reason to be assigned is that it is "prejudicial to health," and for this statement there is every justification. To begin with there is no such thing as pure lymph. It is " septic matter" from a wound, and that too, the wound of a beast, that is subject to special diseases, and every sane man who gives the matter serious consideration would say that to put such vile stuff into a human body is prejudicial enough. But, I forgot ; objectors to the rite of vaccination are not sane. They are, according to the Mayor, " lunatics." All I can say is that there are some illustrious " lunatics " in the country, The letest " lunatic "of note is no less a personage than the Rev. Hugh Price Hughes. Listen te what he says :—" As the son of a medical man, I was brought up after the straightest sect of those who believe in vaccination, and I myself have often been vaccinated. Of late years, however, I have been compelled to study the question, and the facts produced before the Royal Commission have finally convinced me that vaccination is a great mistake, and that compulsory vaccination is one of the most fearful outrages of sacred human right that selfish- ness and cowardice have ever devised." That, mind you, is from a man who has studied the subject, and when our magistrates, and the Mayor in particular, also study it, / will underteke to say, that they will give like testimony, and that we shall see no more of the childish exhibitions we have had of late.—Yours &amp;c..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>REFORMER.</head>
        <p>A REFORMER.</p>
        <p>The Sad Fatality to a Servant Girl at Grappenhall.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l fear that you are comtituting yourself both Judge and Jury in the matter pertaining to the death of this poor girl, and ignoring the fact toat between us there is a discerning and discreet public to whose opin ion I arn quitp willing to submit. In your last week's remarks you kindly stated for the information of myself and the public that the poor girl's neck was broken. In this week's issue you say referring to my latter. " He also has had information from" a reliable source" with regard to the assertion that the girl's neck was broken." May I be permitted to ask the public to read my letter, wherein I state as plainly as I could find language to do so. "That the poor girl's neck was not broken" in contradiction to your statement of the previous welc.</p>
        <p>As to the letter of Mr. L. .1( ffa who appears on one ! occasion to have occupied the responsible position of Jurist, and who at that time using his own words—" I have always found it nur dutv to consider th. eviience and pass our own verdict."—i dare say Mr. Mrs means something, but I am sorry that to me WI meaning is in-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, DECEMBER 15. 1899</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, DECEMBER 15. 1899</p>
        <p>explicable. Probably tbere are not many L. Jeffs in the County,and any Police-Constable might have very much trouble in summoning on a jury twelve young men possessing the natural ability which he does, and hence they are driven to the alternative of selecting elderly men to form our local juries, and I believe the same course is pursued in our courts of jurisprudence. If am correctly inf. irmed Mr. L. Jeffs has written to the Chief Constable of the Couety,and to the Superiii,2ndent of Police, as well as to yourself, on the question of his sister's death, and against the wish of his poor afflicted father. What end he may have in view, or what purpose he may be serving by such conduct it is hard to conjecture. As to the ver- dict : The family lam sure entertain no thought of suicide, and there is no reason to believe that the poor young girl's life was taken from her, and I repeat the jury bad no evidence and coald have n e:e eitiier from gardener, policeman, or doctor barring a post-mortem Which must have shown deai h from injury caused by the fall or suffocation by droning, auu if the former had been proved to have been the cause of death instead of the latter, little difference would have been made in the position occupied by the bereaved father ar,cl sorrow- ing femily, for wismal the Coroner and the jury could not but express their deepest sorrow and sympathy. I thank you for you courtesy in inserting my explana- tions as a member of the jury and must cease further correspondence thereon after date.—Yours obediently, STEPHEN H. JENKINS. December 6th.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jenkins persistently misunderstands us, and in charging us indirectly with misquoting him he shows a further misconception of the Queen's English. We stated last week that Mr. Jenkins 12.1,1 "also had in- formation from a reliable source with regard to the assertion that the girl's neck was broken. The two words in italies seem to have escaped Mr. Jenkins' ob. serration, henee his renewed and unnecessary dis- claimer. We are glad that our correspondent has decided to withdraw from the discussion, for it is very unlike:y that he would have got any nearer the mark had he written for a year. Our original, and sole con- tention was that there was an insufficiency of evidence called at the inquest—particularly medical evidence— and that contention we still uphold. Neither do we think Mr. Jenkins has advanced anything to rebut it. an I we are confident that "the discerning and discrtet public " will be of similar opinion. Mr. Ernest Summer., Warrington, the young mna deceased was engaged to. has also written us exoressing his dissatisfnotion at what he terms •• the rag-ends of evidence" brought forward at the inquest. He is also ef the opinion that medical testimony should have been heard.</p>
        <p>Having raised our protest against the nature of the Coroner's enquiry we are satisfied, and believing that no ge id can oe derived from further correspondence, we have decided that it must cease with this issue.—" ED Ex."</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON. NEWTON MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT SOCIETY. At the weekly meeting of this Society on Monday evening the Rev. P. R. Passmore, read a paper on "Charles Kings!ey. The essay wao interesting and instructive,and wes much enjoyed by those present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>EARLESTOWN HOMING SOCIETY. Prize Distribution.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN HOMING SOCIETY. Prize Distribution.</p>
        <p>The annual dinner ia connection with this Society wa, held ist the Griffin Ro-.81, E,alestown on Saturday even- ing. Abo,.t 40 persons sat down to au excellent spread, lull justice being done to the good things provided. After the cloth had been removed, the proceedings were presided over by Mr A Skeen in the unavoidable absence f Mr Rachel the President, who, however, arrived later on in the evening. There wero a number of Fanciers present from various distric's, including Mr Edt,eley (Penketb) Messrs Slater aad Hill of Blackorook, etr aloncrLiff (Southport), Mr Tneiwell (Chester) and Councillor Gilmour. After the usual loyal toasts had been honoured, The Beer tary tubmitted his rep )It, in which he Said the membership of the society was 36. a slight decrease of last yr, ar. The members had racea during the past season 1,587 birds, which was very good considering the club was composed principally of small lots. One of their members, J. Abbott, had the honour of winuilg Ist, and 10th in the Federation from Bournemouth (oi birds) in whie‘i 4,257 birds comp, tad. Tie members oh the club had als ) gained six o her p &amp;ion tin the firsr twentyii the Fodera.ion, a ye y eac 11-1 r cord seeing tha-, th3Federati .n was coin!. 03 d fl 6 clubs. As re- gards finsrtai, they started the season with a balance in hand of 16s 91 and after paying £2O 158 6d. in prize for that night, and two cups %slue £7 17- 61 unainz a t ital of £3O 13s Ott they ha! a bal nee in ha, dor £2 19s 4d tie thought they sv,re wuc.. ia ebt3d to the following g-ntlemea for special priz s duriug the past s.asoo, Maisra. Lou :ton, Slack, 0 ula!dgon, J. Lythgo3 Gibson, Gilmour, Burrows, and Dr. Piti ono°. Mr- H. J. Longton afrerwards preselted the prizes as follow :—J Abbott, 3r I, Swindon to Eartstown old birds ; Ist and 2ud, Bournemouth ; 2nd, Guernrey; Ist, Swindim, young birth ;£4 143 4i. J Ashcroft, 2:id, windon old birds ; Ist, Guernsey ; 1 ,1, Pio-lime Stakes (Swindon young birds) £4 143 4d J Lychq,oe, Ist Hants. Ist Gloucester (young birds) £3 33 4i. J Apple- ton. Ist an 1 3rd Jetev £1 8 61. Hall and Naylor, Ist Swindmi (old birds) 3rd,Swindon (young birds) £l. 8 61. A sk ee,2n I. Haut,' (2 old 6ird.i) 2nd Gloucester (yon. birds) £l3, 61. T Lyth,loe, 2ad,Produce Stakes (Ssviri- don) Tann!, bed. ogb n Bros., 2nd Jersey, us 61. R Bart ay, 3rd. Hants lie 4d. T Ball, 2nd Sorindon, young birds, us 61. S Dare, 3rd Bournemouth old birds, Es 81. A silver cup value £3 was presented to Mr. J. Lythgoe for best average in young bird races. Mr. J. Appleton won the silver tea service presented by Mr. Longron for best average velocity in old and young bird races.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Beatty was present,A with a gold brooch in recog- nition of her servicei in connection with the club.</p>
        <p>Dosing the evening songs were given by Messrs. W. Gerrard and Mr. Lawreoson, and a stump spent] by Mr W. Hearty. on " Temperance created much amusement. Mr. T E. Cough rfciteci " The Abseat-Minded Beggar," Master T. Beatty sang "The Slier 4 of tt”, Que-n," after wWch a c Ilectiin was made in aid of tin " Racing Pigeon War Fund, £ll9. being realised. fla, gathering, irliCh had been mos-. esjoy‘ble was 'Nought to a close by the usu vo-,e3 of ht,lg4.</p>
