      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY LECTURES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY LECTURES.</p>
        <p>"Lancashire Song Writers and Poets."</p>
        <p>1 Mr. S. Pope was the chairman at the third lecture of the series delivered in connection with the Runcorn Free Library, at the Technical Institute, Runcorn, on Monday evening. The lecturer was Mr. A. Lancaster I(chi,f librarian of St. Helens) and he dealt with " Lancashire song writers and poets," which was his subject, in a masterful manner. In all ages and countries poetry, songs and ballads had been recording the earliest I acts and events in history. Early Britain, bad its bards and minstrels who wrote and sang in praise of their heroes—the living warriors of their day—so that they might be encouraged to emulate the deeds of their fore- fathers. It aas a striking fact that almost the whole of the early poetry of England had for its theme—war, and it was not till later, through a refinement of manners, that a wider range of subjects was entered upon. These latter embraced domestic lore, dearness of home, senti- ment of passion, and religious themes. Following came the satirical song, the humorous and burlesque ballad which were sung in the halls of the great by minstrels of the "North Countrie," and which for many generations formed the fireside melodies of the peasantry. The history of any striking event was put into doegrel verse by a ballad-monger, and these were carried from ' place to place by himself or committed to the charge of the itinerant pedlar. Of the Lancashire poems the great charm which many of them passessed ve 5 certainly not surpas-ed by those written in pu, ei grammatical English. Rather the homeliness of many of them had obtained for them lasting p ppularity because they were written in the everyday language of I the people, and referred to local usages' customs and , superstitions. About the time John Collier, better I known as "Tim Bobbin" published " Tummus and Mary" Lancashire poets first commenced to write to any appreciable extent poems in their Lancashire dialect. And following that initial attempt there were many ' others until at the present there were in "folk speech I some of the finest and noblest effusions ever penned. When contemplating the meritorious efforts of Bamford, Brierley, Laycock, Waugh and others they were com- pelled to admire the siarple and in many cases the pathetic poems which had a diitinctive beauty entirely their own and were truly "diamonds in the rough." Should a Londoner be brought into contact with a few dialect talkers he would he astonished beyond measure and would wonder what manner of language her Majesty' subjects were spedring. But if he were told this jargon, so unmusical in general conversation, was the rep Isitory of some of the moat exquisite melodies and gems of domestic poetry in the English language heard, scout the idea as preposterous. Yet such was the truth, nor could it be refuted. The anthology of Lancashire was very extensive and varied, anti it could be safely said that its poetical literature was represented by more than 100 authors, many of whom had com- menced their careers by contributing their "first things" to the current periodical publications of the time. Hay- leg referred to the works and life of Richard Sheade, a 116th century poet ; Prince, the composer of" A Father's Lament ;" William Shenstone. John Byrom, Samuel Bamford, Charles Swain, .1 B Rogerson, W H Ainsworth (better known as a novelist than a poet), W Rawlinson, Mrs G Bankes, Mrs Hemans, Samuel Laycock, Ben Brierley, Richard Bealey, Joseph Ramabottom, J Shuttle Dawson, and Wilson, and others the lecturer concluded ' with the hope that his remarks would do something to bring these treasures more forcibly before the reading public of Runcorn.—Mr. F Hutton proposed and Mr. W Elandley seconded a hearty vote of thanks to the lecturer, which was accorded, and the meeting dis- persed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>ENVELOPES FROM 2s. PER 1,000.</head>
        <p>ENVELOPES FROM 2s. PER 1,000.</p>
        <p>44EXAMINER " STEAM PRINTING WORKS,</p>
        <p>BRWSEY STREW, WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>REV. F. C. SHIRTCLIFFE.</head>
        <p>REV. F. C. SHIRTCLIFFE.</p>
        <p>This gentleman who has resigned his curacy of St. John's Church, Earlastown, has we understand, accepted a similar appointment at Great Crosby. Mr. Shirtcliffa during his connection with Earles- town, has by his unassuming manner,made himself very popular with the parishioners, and his im- pending departure is regretted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>ODDFELLOW'S BALL AT BARLBSTOWN.</head>
        <p>ODDFELLOW'S BALL AT BARLBSTOWN.</p>
        <p>A most buccessful ball took place in the Town Earlestown on Friday evening under the aus- pices of the Loyal Viaduct lodge of Oddfellows, M.U. The hall had been very nicely decorated by Mr. A. Clarke, and with about 50 couples present the scene was an animated one. The music was supplied by Mr. Sudworth's string band, and danc- ing was kept up until about 4 o'clock on t'aturday morning, the MAL% being Messrs. Pill and Banton. The catering for the refreshment of the inner man was efficiently carried out by Mr. G. E !wards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>ACCIDENT TO AN EARLESTOWN FOOIBALLICR.</head>
        <p>ACCIDENT TO AN EARLESTOWN FOOIBALLICR.</p>
        <p>During the p rogess of the game between Earles- own and Adling ton, in the first round of the, Wigan cup competition, on Saturday at Adlington " Shut "Parr the Earlestown centre half back.bad to be assisted off the field through an injury to his leg. He had later to be removed home in a cab, and it now transpires that a small bone has been broken which will necessitate his absence from the field of play, for tbe remainder of the season. This will cause much regret to the supporters of Earlestown, as he was a valuable player in the half back divi- sion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>P.S.A.</head>
        <p>P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly meeting of the Earlestown and District P.S.A. was held in the Co-opervive Hall. Earlestown on Sunday afternoon. Mr A. Morris pre- sided, and there was a large attendance. Mr Henry Taylor, Police Court Missionary of Warrington gave a most interesting address in which he recounted many incidents which had come to his knowledge during his career as a Missionary. Recitations were given by Mr Huffer, and vocal selections were given by Miss Marion Holt. Mrs Latimer presided at the piano.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>PRIDE OF EARLESTOWN TONTINE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>PRIDE OF EARLESTOWN TONTINE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The nineteenth annual general meeting of the above Society was held at the Viaduct Hotel, Earlestown, on Saturday evening, Mr. A. Greet- holder presiding. Mr. C. And lay, the secretary, in submitting his report to the members, said he was very sorry owing to the great amount of sicknef;s and death occurring to members, they were not in a position to grant any division of the funds as in former years, but hoped they would continue to work together and have better health next year 7 The receipts this year amounting to £6O 4s 4d were distributed as follows :—ln sickness and death, £5B 59 2d; Management Fund, £lO 12s 6d. The deficiency being withdrawn from the reserve fund, leaving the balance of the reserve tund to the Society at the substantial figure of £36 12s. The elec'ion of officers then took place. Mr. Great- holder was re-elected chairman ; Mr. Sharp. treasurer, Mr. C. Audly, secretary. After a social and enjoyable evening the proceedings were brought to a close by singing the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLARY.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARY.</p>
        <p>The Hope of Rarlestown Lodge held their meet- ing in the Co-operative Hall, Warfzrave-road on Monday, Bro Cutler, C.T. presiding. After the open- biz services two candidates were proposed for in- itiation. Bro Woods,L.D. read cosnmunications from Good Templar Orphanage for subscriptions and from Grand Secretary asking the members to take up a collection at the Christman dinner table in aid of the Home Mission Fund. It was resolved to take up a collection next week for the Orphanage, the Home Mission Fund to be left in Bro Wood's charge. Bro Newman gave the report of the Convention hold at St. Helens. Bro Cutler, C.T. gave the report of special committee for the New Year's social. A note was read from Fro Clark who suggested that the proceeds should be sent to the War Fund. Sis Warburton pointed out that it was very likely there would be no balance as it was got up for the mem- bers to enjoy themselves and not for profit. The report recommended that each member be allowed to purchase two tickets, that tickets be sold only by a few members, and that no tickets be sold after the Tuesday preceding the party. It was resolved that the proceeds of the Limelight Rxhibition to be held (Sn February 15th be given to the War Fund. The Lodge was closed by Bro Cutler, CT., the atten- dance being the largest this season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>MEETnG OF THE PAROCFML COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Sco;‘kr,on Hea,th Parochial Committee was held in the National Snhoolroom on Thursday even- ing. Mr. Davenport prea-led. mad th3re were also pre- sent :—M,ssrs. J Whitley, J H G Piling, F Pear- son, J Mclntyre, Mas-in ; with G F Aqaton (clerk). J Swinton surveyor), and J Farrington (nuisance inspec- tor).</p>
        <p>THE NEW SAN'ITAEtY SYSTEM.</p>
        <p>The Town Clerk of Warrington (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) replying to the hquiry of the committee stated that the C ,rporation paid tor pill covers for wooden pails from 8i 61 to 9i each.</p>
        <p>It was decided to ail{ the Corporation at what price they would supply the committee with covers and the subject was lett in toe.h.nds_of a stnall_committee.</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED LIG FIT RAILWAY. STRONG OPPOSITION.</p>
        <p>The Clerk renorte I that I,ar. Joseph H. Clarke had given him written intic3 that it was his intention to propose that the Rural District Council of Runcorn he asked to oppose the or 'posed light railway through the streets of Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>Mr. Clarke said he thought that it was the duty of th- Roads Committee of Stockton Heath to strongly recommend the Runcorn Council to oppose the light railway going through Stockton Heath. In the first place they ought to put themselves in a leeval position to be able to oppose it. There were many r.asons why Stockton 11ath ought not to be saddled with this light railway. They had great obstructions to traffic between Stockton Heath and Warrington. There was the Ship Canal bridge to be crossed and the level crnssing at Wilders- pool. They had an instance of the difficulty experienced with reference to the Ship Canal Bridge that week.when it was open for half-an-hour aeain-t the public. If this light railway was sanctioned they would probably have an illimitable number of trucks running through the s'reets with a large amount of goods 11/Iffic. Time was t m precious in these days for them to be hanging about waiting for railway trains to pass the streets. There was also a great danger to life and property. They weuld have a light railway at their front doer, and they could imagine what the result would he in front of that schoolroom when four hun- dred children might be leaving, and a .rail- way train rushing along the street, probably without any regulations as to speed. It was a very serious matter for Stockton Heath. It might look a small matter but in the course of a very few years Stockton Heath and Warrington would pre- bably be one, and they would then have this light rail way in the town of Warrington. The railway might be of benefit to some of the country districts but Stoekton He .th would derive no tenefit from it. The people would travel through to Warrington. The Ctairman : It will be a great nuisance. Mr. Clarke : We shall have to put up with a great nuisance in order to accammodate people coming out of the country. There were many other reasons but be thought he had given sufficient to justify the passing of the resolution asking the Rural Council of Runcorn to take the matter up. Mr. Filling: Have you any idea as to what motive power is to be used on this Northwich to Warrington railway ? M... Clarke: The Company say in their application that they intend to use electricity hut they will have the option to use other power. There is nothiag to bled the Company to electricity. Mr. Whitley seconded and th e resolution was unanim ously carried.</p>
        <p>£,4000,000 HAVE NOW BEEN PAID IN RESPECT OF RAILWAY ACCIDENTS. ALL ACCIDENTS, WORKMEN'S ACCIDENTS FIDELITY GUARANTEE, BY THE RAILWAY PASSENGERS' ASSURANCE CO. 64, CORNIIILL, LONDON. A. WAN, Secretary. AGENTS required in unrepresented Districts. Manchester Office : 64. Cross Street. AGENTS:</p>
        <p>Warrington Mr. C. J. HOLMES, Market Gate „ Mr. W.H. RlGBEY,Nesscliffe, Victoria-rd.,Latchford St. Helens Mr. J. B. LEACH, Hardshaw-street K kut,efo-d • Sandbach Mr. F.J. FINLOW. Rookery Bridge Farnworth Mr. J. HOLLAND WATSON,Mot4rfield House</p>
