      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>LORD METHUEN'S REVERSE.</p>
        <p>How The Highlanders Were Caught.</p>
        <p>Desperate Fighting. --- Stubborn Gallantry of The Boers.</p>
        <p>MODDER RIVER CAMP, TI7E3DAY MORNING.</p>
        <p>Yesterday's battle was the most desperate of tne series which this army has fought since it started to drive the enemy from its front. Our immediate object failed ; our losses have been terribly severe, bet the struggle brought into bold relief all tne best qualities ot the British soldier. We know now th4t the bombardment which we maintained for boars on Sunday and M,,nday upon the Boer trenches inflicted fearful havoc amongst the enemy, but it did not destroy their fine nerve, al- though it killed and wounded hundreds of them. We marched during the night upon Magersfontein, and wro hopeful of catching the Boers napping. The High- land Brigade led the advance, marching in close order. It was thought that the nearest of the Boer re nches were a long way off. Just as day was breaking, however, the Highlanders almost stumbled upon the enemy's advanced trenches. They bad got within short rifle range, and then the con- cealed Boers opened fire with appalling effect into our close ranks, men falling by the score at every volley. Ihe Hiplanders fell back, took wh tt cover they c and waited, still under a tremen leue fire until support was sent for. Then they made another frontal attack, only to fail again. Meantime the Guards' Brieade were endeavouring to turn the enemy's left flak, bet the positions held by the B )ers were virtually impregnable, and meveover the enemy had been reinforced at that part of the wide field of battle by the arrival, in the small hours of the morningof 1,000 fresh men and guns. The flanking movement bad therefore to be abandoned. Our artillery fire throughout the day was magnificent. Tee naval 4-7 inch gun, which our fellows have named "Joe Chamberlain," was conspicuous throughout the battle, and did superb service; and next in activity and efficiency was the howitz3r bittery. These euns terew big shells charged with lyddite right ainengst the Boers for hours. Every shell fell true and exploded accurately with devastating effect. The Beers foughe win stub- born gallantry from beginr.ing to end, and tor_k advan- tage of every turn of the game. It is abundantly evident that we shall have to turn them out of their positions at the point of the bayonet. Nothing else will move them. The cost will be great, but the work will have to be done. Our heavy casualties include many officers. When night fell we had gained no advantage. This morning the whole force returned to this camp and are now resting.</p>
        <p>Lord Methuen Compelled to Retire.</p>
        <p>" MODDER RIVER, TUESDAY, 7.30 P.M,</p>
        <p>"4s Boors occupied the trenches ttrongly this morn- ing I retired, in perfect order, here, where I am in scud ty. I gather from prisoners and from Boers speaking t 3 men with ambulances that their losses have ha -n terrible, some corps being quite wiped out. They have been moat kind to my wounde I."</p>
        <p>Terrible British Losses. 833 Killed, Wounded, and Missing.</p>
        <p>The Central News says :—The official list shows the the losses among the officers were fifteen killed, forty- eight wounded, five misiing, one prisoner. The casual- ties among the troops outii le the Highland Brigade were eight men killed. 108 wounded and missing. Taking the official estimate of the 1.-,ss3s of the men of the Highland Brigade, the entire casualties in tne engagement amount o 833 officers and men killed, wound cd, missing, and prisoners. In addition to General Wauchope, Major, the Marquis of Winchester (Coldstream Guards), and Lieu- tenant Colonel Coode (Gordon Ilitolan ders) were killed. IS LADYSMITH RELIEVED? Startling Statement.</p>
        <p>The Exchange Company publies the following under all reserve, and on the solo authority of a rumour which has been persistent tor some time on the London Stock Exchange :—" It is stated that General Buller and Clery have relieved Ladysmith and taken 10,000 prisoners. No official or other confirmation whatever ot this start- ing statement is obtainable."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>EVENTS OF THE WEEK. BRITISH DEFEAT AT MAGERSFONTEIN.</head>
        <p>EVENTS OF THE WEEK. BRITISH DEFEAT AT MAGERSFONTEIN.</p>
        <p>Lord MsTilukti wired, Dec. 12 : The artillery shelled very strong position held by thr enemy in long high kopje from tour o'clock to dusk on Sunday, rained hard last night. Highland Brigade attacked at daybreak Monday south end of kopje ; at- tack properly timed. Attack failed. Guards, as ordered prof ected Highland right and rear. Cavalry, Mounted Infantry and Howitzer Artillery battery attacked enemy oa left, Guards ea rig,ht and centre supported by Field Artillery and Ho witzer. Artillery shelled the position from daybreak, and at a quarter past one I sent the Gordons to support of 11 ighland Brigade. Troops held their own in rote, of the enemy's entrenchments until aark, the pesi- ion extending, including the kopj.. a distance of six miles trwarels Molder River. lam to-day Tuesday boiling my position and entrenching myself. I had to face at le rst 12,000. Our loss is</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>General_Wauchope_Killed.</head>
        <p>General_Wauchope_Killed.</p>
        <p>T ,o) General Officer Commaudiog at Capetown, in a later message, says : " Methwm wires General Wauchope killed in action yererday."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-12-15_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Disaster to General Gatacre's Column.</head>
        <p>Disaster to General Gatacre's Column.</p>
        <p>600 Men Captured.</p>
        <p>The anticipatrd fight on Sunday t3ok place in an un- expected quarter,the vicinity of Stormberg Jiinction,and Greneral Gatacre " deeply regrets " to report that he 11,.8 bad a " st-rious reve,s, " EDS fore 3,000 or more strong, including two batteries of artillery, wont by train from Putterfesraal ti Molten° on Saturday and thence by a forced march of a (*Vim miles on Sunday to tho positima occupied by ttie Boers. " A big artillery duel " raged from four o'clock till seven in the morning, hut the enemy were so placed as to b 3 " unassailable by infantry," and after a hot fir had been " unex- pectedly opened on our troops," the British force fell r ack on Molten°. General Gatacre informs the War Office that he was misled as to the position of the enemy by his guides, and that he found the " giound im- praYticable." The British losses are 672 killecl,wounded and missing, over 600 being included in the latter category. They are reported to be captured.</p>
        <p>I</p>
