        <p>SEE NEXT 4 ,0 F RIDAY'S _UXAMINER,'' FOR A SPECIAL CHRISTMAS STORY BY GRANT ALLEN, ENTITLED :__ "A CHRISTMAS ADVENTURE." The following outline of the story will be perused with interest. Lancelot Saville whel cycling .dawn to a e-aintry howls to inset his betrothed, Roma Pendleton meets with an unfortunate aecident in a deserted part of the country, and by breaking his leg is exposed to many dangers. He is in danger ot dying from starvation, of being robbed by tramps, and finally of being locked up as a bad character. Out of these materials, the author has woven a very pleasing story, which should be perused with great interest and pleasure in the family circle. Few writers are better known to tame than the late Mr. orant Allen. He is celebrated both as a scientist and a novelist,and so great is the demand for his work that just before his death this year ha trebled his prices for stories and still the commissions came roiling in from botb sides of the Atlantic. '' A 0 ristinas Adventure," will be found one of his best storyettes. We have also pleasure in announcing a MOSt INTERESTING ARTICLE ON "CONJURING FOR CHRISTMAS " BY PROFESSOR HOFFMAN, ;rho has the reputation cf being the greatest living authority on modern magi°, and this article fully sustains his reputation. The subject of the article Is •• Spirit Slate-writing Tricks.' and it will be embellished by SEVEN ILLUSTRATIONS " E SER 14-'RIDAY'S XAMINER."</p>
        <p>Letters to the L. ut. Runcorn and Electric Light and Power. Sir.—The discussion at the last meeting of Runcorn Urban Council must have caused une onnesA to .thiriking ratepayers. By the practical unanimity of the Council a provisional order has been obtained enabling the Cuu- cii erect an installation for the supply of electricity, and since that important document was placed in the arcuives of the Town Hall, the Council has been divided into two sections—one section anxious to put the order to practical use as quickly as possible arid the second section by devious uses of the standing orders and argu- ments calculated to frighten the timid,nds endeavoured to prevent, (and successfully for the present), any de- finite conclusion being arrived at. Take Thursday's meeting last week. The Highways Committee proposed to engage an expert to investigate and report upon various phases, of the electricity ques- tion as it could be applied to Runcorn, inciuding the important question of a refuse destructor. The sum allocated was 30 guineas. This, so that the ratepayers would have so-ne data to guide them in deciding whether the Council should, like numerous other puolic bodies, embark in the business of produciug elec- tricity and applying it to daily life. The seoend section opposed this on the side issue that the engineer m-ntioned had unnecessarily called the attention of the cianinittee to something they had overiooked,natnely a refuse destruc- tor and offering to also inquire into and rsport upon tb, feasibility of providing this in conjunction with an elec- tric installation ; and proposed that another engineer, whose fee was 25 guineas should have the job. Bur, mark you, the report as to refuse destructor was eli- minated from the minute. After a discussion during which every standing order ruling debate was broken, the party of fiAt styled economists carried tbeir point and the twenty-five guinea , man was appointed and the refuse destructor was dropped. Now amongst all the nuisances in Runcorn refuse removal and disposal causes oue of the greatest and in summer time the neighbourhood of Doctors' Bridge is not fit for a person to live in ; and yet these gentlemen for the sake of five guineas decline to accept a minute which might in the end lead to a s ,lution. But was the five guineas the real cause of the opposition ? . Municipal trading is now approved and advocated in high places and there can be no reasonable douot it is to the advantage of the people to possess control of their own water, light tramways, he., and Runcorn if it is to develop and receive due return from the rates paid must not be a laggard in following the spirt of the times. The public at the present time do not get value for the high rate of 3s 3d which they pay and this is a statement easy of proof. Yours truly, LOOKER ON.</p>
